window.GEAR_BOOK_CONTENTS["book-33"] = [{"id":1,"rawContent":"# 序曲：立在田埂上的老刺竹篙\n\n秋風掠過烏溪南岸的平原，整片金黃色的稻浪像是被一雙無形的巨大手掌撫過，發出窸窸窣窣的摩擦聲，伴隨著一股濃郁乾爽的成熟穀香。\n\n在臺中市烏日區溪尾國小的操場圍籬旁，一群身穿運動服的五、六年級學生，正把幾塊空地圍得水洩不通。\n\n「哇，好粗！這真的是竹子嗎？我兩隻手都合不攏耶！」\n\n「你看這裡，紅紅亮亮的，摸起來好滑，像塗了一層油一樣。」\n\n「老師，上面一條一條深深的痕跡是什麼？是刀子刻的嗎？」\n\n孩子們七嘴八舌地驚呼著。在他們中間，橫臥在一對木支架上的，是一根長達五公尺以上、碗口粗細的大刺竹篙。\n\n這根竹子與一般在菜園裡見到的竹竿截然不同。它通體泛著一種被長年日光與流水浸潤後的深栗紅色，表面被打磨得宛如包漿的古木般光潤；但在竹身的中段與下端，卻布滿了一道道交錯縱橫的細密凹痕，那不是刀刻的痕跡，而是成千萬次與粗糙滾燙的麻繩、河底尖銳的卵石，以及暴烈濁流反覆撞擊後所留下的歲月烙印。\n\n在竹篙最前端約莫三十公分處，竹節微微向上自然彎曲，呈現出一個沉穩優雅的弧度。\n\n「那可不是刀痕，是老天爺與大河刻上去的年輪。」\n\n一個清脆而溫和的聲音傳來。留著俐落短髮、穿著沾有些許泥土工作圍裙的年輕女性走了過來。她是林曉棠，溪尾村土生土長的返鄉青年，也是這項「食光穗稻」食農文化計畫的發起人。\n\n曉棠蹲下身，伸手輕輕撫摸著竹篙上最深的一道凹痕，眼神裡流露出一種如同凝視長輩般的敬意。\n\n「曉棠姊姊，這根大竹子到底是做什麼用的啊？操場邊不是有鋼管旗桿嗎，為什麼要留著一根老竹竿放在這裡？」一個理著平頭的男孩好奇地抬頭問。\n\n曉棠微微一笑，抬手指向遠處。\n\n順著她的手指望去，在金黃色稻田的盡頭，橫亙著一道宏偉無比的現代建築——那是二○一六年通車的「溪尾大橋」。紅白相間的鋼拱高聳入雲，平整寬闊的六線道橋面上，車輛如甲蟲般飛馳而過。在晴空萬里之下，從這裡開車穿過大橋抵達烏日市區的繁華街道，只需要短短五分鐘。\n\n「你們看那座大橋，很壯觀對不對？」曉棠問。\n\n孩子們紛紛點頭。\n\n「但是，在你們的阿公、阿嬤，甚至曾祖父小時候，這裡根本沒有橋。別說是水泥橋，就連一根鋼筋都沒有。」曉棠的聲音放得很輕，卻清晰地傳進每個孩子的耳朵裡，「那時候的溪尾寮，被兩條大河——烏溪跟貓羅溪，像一把巨大的剪刀一樣狠狠剪開。對北邊的臺中人來說，這裡是被遺忘的『內陸澎湖』，是一座四面環水的孤島。」\n\n「那……如果沒有橋，生病要看醫生怎麼辦？肚子餓要買東西怎麼辦？」平頭男孩眼睛瞪得圓圓的。\n\n「還有，小孩子怎麼去上學？」另一個綁著馬尾的女生跟著追問。\n\n「就靠它。」\n\n曉棠的手掌重重拍在沉重的大刺竹上，發出一聲厚實的悶響。\n\n「這根竹篙，在以前的溪尾村有一個響噹噹的名字，叫做『定波篙』。五十多年前，每天清晨天還沒亮，當整個村子還沉睡在濃霧與河水咆哮聲中時，村裡十幾個跟你們一樣大的孩子，就得背著帆布書包，站在河邊搖晃的竹筏上。河水深不見底，浪頭又急又兇，只要竹筏一翻，人就會被捲進大肚溪的泥流裡。」\n\n說到這裡，圍觀的孩子們不由自主地屏住了呼吸，有人下意識地低頭看了看自己腳下平坦乾燥的水泥地。\n\n「那時候，有一位老人。他手裡就握著這根竹篙，站在竹筏的最前端。不管夏天暴雨山洪有多大，不管冬夜寒風多麼刺骨，只要村民在對岸點起火把喊一聲：『過溪喔——』他就會毫不猶豫地推筏下水，一篙一篙，把生病的人送到醫院，把採收的穀子與西瓜運到市集，把背著書包嚇得發抖的孩子，平平安安渡到對岸的學校。」\n\n曉棠的眼眶微微發亮，她望向身後那一望無際、沉甸甸垂著穗子的稻田。\n\n「我的父親阿順，小時候是全村最怕水的小孩。他只要看到浪花就會發抖，連站上竹筏的勇氣都沒有。但就是那位手握竹篙的老擺渡人，牽著他的手，教他怎麼在大浪裡看清前方的山頭，教他怎麼借著水流的力量站穩腳步。」\n\n一陣秋風吹過，稻穗如金色波浪般翻湧起伏，空氣中彷彿飄來了數十年前大河泥漿的腥鹹，以及老竹筏划破水面的破水聲。\n\n「那位老擺渡人叫阿榮伯。他是烏日鄉公所公聘的擺渡人，一輩子沒有離開過這條河。」曉棠輕輕握住竹篙的末端，轉頭看著眼前這群生長在便利時代的孩子們，「今天，我們要一起下田割稻，也要親手紮一艘竹筏。但在我們動手之前，我想把阿榮伯、我父親阿順，還有這條大河的故事，講給你們聽。」\n\n遠處的溪尾大橋上傳來汽車掠過的風切聲，與近處稻浪的沙沙聲奇妙地重疊在一起。\n\n那是時間的迴聲。在記憶的長河裡，大橋的鋼骨漸漸隱去，平整的水泥堤防悄然退後，咆哮翻滾的黃色濁浪再次漫過無垠的荒灘……\n\n故事，要從五十多年前那個春耕的清晨，以及那句「種一條菜瓜，藤旋三縣市」的老俗話說起。","rawContentEn":"# Prologue: The Ancient Thorny Bamboo Pole Standing on the Ridge\n\nThe autumn wind swept across the plains on the southern bank of the Wu River, and the endless expanse of golden rice waves rustled as if caressed by a colossal, invisible hand, releasing a rich, crisp aroma of ripe grain.\n\nBeside the playground fence of Xiwei Elementary School in Wuri District, Taichung City, a crowd of fifth and sixth graders in athletic uniforms gathered tightly around an open patch of ground.\n\n\"Whoa, it's so thick! Is this really bamboo? I can't even get both hands around it!\"\n\n\"Look here—it's reddish, glossy, and so slick to the touch, like it was coated in oil.\"\n\n\"Teacher, what are those deep, jagged grooves all over it? Were they carved with a knife?\"\n\nThe children chattered in astonishment. Resting horizontally between them upon a pair of wooden trestles was a massive thorny bamboo pole, over five meters long and as thick as a soup bowl.\n\nThis bamboo was entirely unlike any ordinary garden stake. Its entire body radiated a deep chestnut-crimson hue, steeped by decades of relentless sunlight and river water, polished as smooth as heirloom lacquered wood. Yet along its midsection and lower end lay a dense network of crisscrossing indentations. Those were no deliberate knife carvings; they were the indelible brands of time, etched by tens of thousands of violent collisions against coarse, scalding hemp ropes, jagged riverbed boulders, and furious muddy currents.\n\nRoughly thirty centimeters from its tip, the bamboo's nodes gently arched upward in a natural, dignified curve.\n\n\"Those aren't knife marks. They are the growth rings carved by the heavens and the great river.\"\n\nA clear, gentle voice rang out. A young woman with brisk short hair, wearing a canvas work apron smudged with fine soil, walked over. She was Lin Xiaotang, a hometown youth born and raised in Xiwei Village, and the founder of this agricultural-cultural program called \"The Ferryman's Golden Grain.\"\n\nXiaotang knelt down, gently running her fingers over the deepest groove on the bamboo pole, her eyes filled with the quiet reverence one might reserve for an elder.\n\n\"Sister Xiaotang, what was this giant bamboo pole actually used for? We have steel flagpoles right by the playground, so why keep an old bamboo pole here?\" a boy with a buzz cut asked, looking up in curiosity.\n\nXiaotang smiled faintly and pointed into the distance.\n\nFollowing her finger, at the far horizon of the golden rice fields, stood a magnificent modern structure—the Xiwei Bridge, inaugurated in 2016. Its red-and-white steel arches soared into the sky, while vehicles darted like beetles across its smooth, expansive six-lane deck. Under a cloudless sky, driving across this bridge to the bustling avenues of downtown Wuri took a mere five minutes.\n\n\"You see that bridge? Magnificent, isn't it?\" Xiaotang asked.\n\nThe children nodded in unison.\n\n\"Yet, when your grandfathers, grandmothers, and even your great-grandparents were little, there was no bridge here at all. Not a concrete bridge, not even a single steel rebar,\" Xiaotang's voice softened, yet fell with unmistakable clarity into the ears of every child. \"Back then, Xiwei Village was severed as if by a pair of giant shears wielded by two mighty rivers—the Wu River and the Maoluo River. To the people of Taichung to the north, this place was an abandoned 'inland Penghu,' an isolated enclave surrounded entirely by water.\"\n\n\"Then... if there was no bridge, what did people do when they got sick and needed a doctor? What if they ran out of food and had to buy groceries?\" the buzz-cut boy asked, his eyes wide.\n\n\"And how did the children get to school?\" another girl with a ponytail chimed in.\n\n\"They depended on this.\"\n\nXiaotang's palm struck the heavy thorny bamboo with a solid, resounding thud.\n\n\"In the old Xiwei Village, this pole had a legendary name: the 'Wave-Calming Pole.' More than fifty years ago, before dawn broke each morning—while the whole village still slept beneath heavy fog and the roar of rushing rapids—a dozen children just like you had to shoulder their canvas backpacks and balance upon a rocking bamboo raft by the riverbank. The water was bottomlessly deep, the whitecaps fierce and unforgiving. If the raft tipped, you'd be swallowed straight into the muddy torrents of the Dadu River.\"\n\nHearing this, the listening children held their breath involuntarily; a few glanced down subconsciously at the dry, level concrete under their feet.\n\n\"In those days, there was an old man. Holding this very pole, he stood at the prow of the raft. No matter how fierce the summer flash floods raged, or how bitterly the winter gales howled, the moment a villager on the opposite bank lit a torch and cried out, 'Crossing the river!' he would push the raft into the dark water without a second thought. Stroke by stroke, punt by punt, he carried the critically ill to hospitals, ferried harvested grain and watermelons to market, and safely delivered trembling, terrified schoolboys across to the classrooms on the other shore.\"\n\nXiaotang's eyes glistened slightly as she looked out at the boundless paddies behind her, heavy with drooping golden heads of rice.\n\n\"My father, A-Shun, was the most water-fearing child in the entire village when he was little. The mere sight of surging whitecaps made him shiver; he lacked even the courage to step onto a raft. Yet it was that old ferryman, holding this pole, who took him by the hand, teaching him how to fix his eyes on the distant mountain peaks through the spray, and how to harness the river's own force to keep his footing firm.\"\n\nAn autumn breeze swept through, stirring the grain into shimmering golden billows. The air seemed to carry a phantom scent from decades past—the mineral tang of river silt and the crisp rush of an aged bamboo raft cleaving the surface.\n\n\"That old ferryman was called Uncle Rong. He was a ferryman officially commissioned by the Wuri Township Office, and he never left this river his entire life,\" Xiaotang held the end of the pole gently, turning to face this generation raised in modern convenience. \"Today, we are going to step barefoot into the muddy paddies to harvest rice, and we will lash together a real bamboo raft with our own hands. But before we begin, I want to tell you the story of Uncle Rong, of my father A-Shun, and of this mighty river.\"\n\nFrom the distant Xiwei Bridge came the faint hum of tires slicing through the wind, blending in wondrous harmony with the nearby rustle of the golden rice waves.\n\nIt was an echo of time. In the river of memory, the steel ribs of the bridge slowly dissolved, the smooth concrete dikes receded into the mist, and the roaring, churning yellow silt once again spilled across the boundless alluvial wild...\n\nThe story begins on an early spring morning more than fifty years ago, with an old village proverb: \"Plant a single melon vine, and its tendrils wind through three counties.\""},{"id":2,"rawContent":"# 第一章：春水微茫——種一條菜瓜，藤旋三縣市\n\n民國五十年代初春的清晨，濃重的白霧宛如浸透了冷水的薄紗，一層層籠罩在溪尾寮廣袤的荒灘與水田上。\n\n十一歲的李順安蹲在自家三合院後方的泥土地上，手裡握著一根削尖的竹籤，聚精會神地撥弄著幾顆黑亮飽滿的絲瓜種子。\n\n在他身後，外公陳水生正挽起褲管，將一桶剛從豬圈起出的發酵堆肥拌進黑土裡。那泥土散發著一股濃烈而溫暖的氣味，深黑、黏稠，像是吸飽了整座中央山脈所有的養分。\n\n「阿順啊，籽埋下去莫埋得太深，留三指寬的軟土給它透氣。」水生伯直起腰，用那隻滿是厚繭、指甲縫嵌著黑泥的手擦了擦額頭上的汗水，笑呵呵地看著外孫，「這溪尾的泥土是烏溪從高山上沖下來的好黑泥，肥得流油。只要你肯下力氣，種下一粒籽，它就能還你一整棚又長又甜的菜瓜。」\n\n阿順小心翼翼地把泥土覆蓋在種子上，用掌心輕輕拍實，隨後仰起小臉問道：「阿公，學校隔壁那個從彰化嫁過來的嬸婆常說：『種一條菜瓜，藤旋三縣市。』這到底是什麼意思啊？一條瓜藤再怎麼會爬，還能爬到南投去嗎？」\n\n水生伯聽了忍不住哈哈大笑，缺了一顆門牙的嘴漏出爽朗的風聲。他丟下竹鏟，牽著阿順的手走出竹圍子，朝著村子邊緣的一條長長的水泥大土堤走去。\n\n「傻囝仔，這可不是開玩笑的話，是你阿公我這輩子天天在過的苦日子。」水生伯抬手指著眼前那道足足有兩層樓高、宛如巨龍般橫臥在平原上的灰色長堤，「你看，咱們溪尾寮，就夾在北邊這條烏溪、跟南邊那條貓羅溪的中間。這兩條大河像兩把剪刀，把咱們跟臺中、彰化、南投剪成一塊一塊的碎布。」\n\n「你看那條水溝，」水生伯指著三合院圍牆外的一道淺水渠，「溝這邊是臺中縣烏日鄉，邁過溝去就是彰化縣芬園鄉；再順著這道堤防走過去不到一里地，地契上寫的又是南投縣草屯鎮。當年日本人在昭和十四年、也就是你阿公二十幾歲的時候，為了保護對岸烏日市區和臺中城不被大水淹，硬是修了這座高高的烏溪大堤防。」\n\n說到這裡，水生伯臉上的笑容漸漸收斂，望向大堤的眼神裡浮現出一種難以言說的滄桑與苦澀。\n\n「大堤修好了，北岸的官老爺和鎮上人高枕無憂；可這座大堤，卻把咱們溪尾寮狠狠丟在堤防外面。每逢夏天大水一來，上游合歡山滾下來的山洪排不出去，全灌進咱們這個『大水盆』裡。咱們是臺中的戶籍、彰化的生活圈，但大水一淹，咱們就成了沒人管的孤魂野鬼。這就叫『孤島』，叫『臺中的澎湖』啊！」\n\n阿順聽得懵懵懂懂，但他記得很清楚：去年夏天那一場罕見的暴雨，咆哮的黃水一夜之間淹過了村口的大榕樹，家裡那頭大黃牛被急流捲走時發出的哀鳴，至今還會讓他半夜從惡夢中嚇出一身冷汗。從那時起，只要看見深不見底、奔流翻滾的水面，他的雙腿就會不由自主地發軟。\n\n「水生叔！阿順！快來看，公所送公文來啦！」\n\n一聲洪亮的呼喊穿透了晨霧。村裡的保正（村長）滿頭大汗地騎著一輛破舊的鐵馬，在泥濘的田埂小路上劇烈顛簸而來。他的後座上綁著一捲大紅紙，前車把上還掛著一串劈啪作響的鞭炮。\n\n「什麼天大的好事，大清早放鞭炮？」水生伯揚聲問道。\n\n「烏日鄉公所剛批下來的公文，正式續聘啦！」保正跳下車，氣喘吁吁地擦著臉上的泥漿，語氣裡充滿了難掩的興奮，「續聘阿榮——林權榮，繼續當咱們溪尾村的公聘擺渡人！每個月公所照舊補貼工錢，全村老小渡溪一律免收半分船錢！」\n\n聽到這個消息，周圍田間裡彎腰插秧的農民們紛紛直起腰桿，爆發出一陣由衷的歡呼。對這座與世隔絕的村落來說，公聘擺渡人不僅僅是一個職位，更是全村數百條人命進出外界唯一的血脈。\n\n「走，阿順，跟阿公去渡口看看阿榮伯！」水生伯拉起阿順的手，快步朝著烏溪南岸的渡口走去。\n\n當他們穿過大堤缺口的防汛便道來到河灘時，晨霧正好被初升的旭日撕開了一道裂縫。金紅色的陽光斜斜照在寬闊的河灘上，只見十幾根粗壯無比、散發著青翠光澤的大刺竹整整齊齊地排在沙地上。\n\n在竹林掩映的岸邊，一個身形不高、精瘦結實得如同老樹根般的中年漢子，正光著腳踩在滾燙的沙堆裡。他穿著一身被汗水與泥水反覆浸透而褪色的粗布短衫，皮膚黝黑發亮，兩條手臂上的青筋如老藤般虯結。\n\n那就是林權榮，村裡老少都敬重稱呼的「阿榮伯」。\n\n此刻，阿榮伯的面前正生著一堆悶燒的乾稻草。濃白帶著草香的煙霧中，一簇暗紅色的炭火正吐著熾熱的火苗。阿榮伯雙手握住一根足有碗口粗細的大刺竹尾端，神情嚴肅得如同一位正在鍛造寶劍的鐵匠。\n\n「阿榮啊，恭喜恭喜！公所那邊公文到了，大夥兒這顆心總算是放回肚子裡了！」水生伯笑著上前打招呼。\n\n阿榮伯沒有立刻抬頭，他的眼睛始終死死盯著火堆上那一截漸漸被烤得發熱流油的竹竿。他的額頭上滾落下一顆顆豆大的汗珠，砸在炭火上發出極細微的「嗤嗤」聲。\n\n「水生叔，先別忙著道喜。」阿榮伯的嗓音低沉沙啞，帶著一種常年與大河咆哮對抗後的渾厚，「開春了，雪水下來，溪面一天比一天寬。這舊筏子被去年的石頭撞得七零八落，經不起今年夏天的大汛。我不趁著這幾天晴天把新竹筏趕出來，過幾天孩子們開學，拿什麼渡他們過江？」\n\n說著，阿榮伯手臂上的肌肉驟然暴起。他用肩膀頂住竹竿的中段，雙手猛力將被炭火烤得軟化的竹尾向下扳壓。\n\n「阿順，過來！」阿榮伯忽然轉過頭，目光銳利如電，落在庫管挽得高高、有些不知所措的阿順身上。\n\n阿順嚇了一跳，下意識地想往外公身後躲。他向來有些害怕阿榮伯那張不苟言笑的臉，更害怕這片散發著潮濕腥味的河灘。\n\n「過來，按住這根麻繩，踩住竹頭！」阿榮伯的語氣不容置疑，但眼神深處卻透著一股沉穩的溫度，「你是男孩子，十一歲了，骨頭該長硬了。學著點，這叫『火烤竹尾』。」\n\n在阿公鼓勵的目光下，阿順咬了咬牙，硬著頭皮快步跑上前去。他赤著的腳掌剛踩上沙地，就感受到了炭火的炙熱。他按照阿榮伯的吩咐，使出吃奶的力氣抱住粗壯的竹竿前端，雙腳死死抵住沙坑裡的木樁。\n\n阿榮伯一邊用沾了冷水的濕布迅速擦拭滾燙的竹節，一邊耐心地對阿順說道：「刺竹是所有竹子裡最硬、最耐磨的，但它脾氣也最倔。如果你不拿火把它烤熱、烤透，順著它的筋骨一點一點把它彎成翹頭，它就會在急流裡硬生生被折斷。」\n\n隨著阿榮伯沉穩有力的按壓與冷水的澆淋，那根原本筆直剛硬的大刺竹尾端，竟奇蹟般地彎曲定型，呈現出一個昂揚上翹的弧度，宛如一隻即將展翅破浪的水鳥胸膛。\n\n「看見沒有？」阿榮伯抹了一把臉上的灰燼，指著彎曲的竹尾說道，「這頭翹起來，推下水的時候，竹筏才能在浪尖上滑過去，而不是一頭栽進旋渦裡被大浪給吞了。人也是一樣，阿順。」\n\n阿榮伯伸出一隻布滿老繭、粗糙得像砂紙般的大手，輕輕拍了拍阿順瘦削的肩膀。那隻手沉重而溫暖，帶著一股太陽曝曬與江水淘洗後的雄渾力量。\n\n「聽你阿公說，你自從去年發大水，就嚇得不敢靠近河邊，甚至連看一眼急流都會打擺子，是也不是？」阿榮伯直視著阿順的眼睛。\n\n阿順的臉瞬間漲得通紅，羞愧地低下了頭，雙手絞著衣角，不敢作聲。\n\n「怕水，不是丟人的事。」阿榮伯的聲音放柔和了些，他抬起頭，望向眼前那片浩浩蕩蕩、向西奔流的灰色大河，「大自然的力量比人不知道大上幾千倍、幾萬倍。面對這條烏溪，不懂得害怕的人，早就被沖到海裡餵魚了。」\n\n阿順驚訝地抬起頭，看著這位傳奇的老船夫。\n\n「但你得記住，害怕歸害怕，你的腳下不能虛。」阿榮伯指向遠處北岸隱約可見的大肚山脈，「這條河把咱們困在這兒，但它隔不開人的心。再過幾天，學校開學，你就算再怕，也得坐著阿伯這艘新竹筏渡過去上課。等到了水中央，要是害怕，你就死死盯著大肚山，只要山在那裡，船就不會翻，心就不會亂。」\n\n阿順順著阿榮伯的手指望去。遠方的大肚山在初春的薄霧中若隱若現，如同一道青藍色的屏障，橫亙在濁浪奔騰的北岸。\n\n他低下頭，看著腳下那根被火烤彎、即將成為新竹筏骨架的大刺竹。竹身上的青皮被火烤成了油亮的暗黃色，摸上去依然散發著熾熱的餘溫。\n\n在這一刻，十一歲的農家少年阿順心中，那股對大河深藏已久的恐懼之中，悄悄生出了一絲從未有過的、想要握住那根定波篙的渴望。","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 1: Faint Spring Waters—Plant a Melon, Its Tendrils Sweep Three Counties\n\nOn an early spring morning in the 1960s, a heavy white mist draped over the vast alluvial flats and flooded paddies of Xiwei Village like layers of gauze drenched in cold water.\n\nEleven-year-old Li Shun'an crouched on the dirt ground behind his family's courtyard compound, holding a sharpened bamboo stick, intently prodding a few plump, glossy black luffa seeds.\n\nBehind him, his grandfather, Chen Shuisheng, rolled up his trouser legs and mixed a bucket of freshly harvested compost from the pigpen into the dark silt. The soil gave off a pungent yet warm odor—deep black and viscously rich, as though it had absorbed every ounce of nourishment shed by the entire Central Mountain Range.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, don't bury the seeds too deep; leave three fingers' width of soft soil so they can breathe.\" Uncle Shuisheng straightened his back and wiped the sweat from his forehead with a thick-callused hand, black dirt embedded under his fingernails. He beamed down at his grandson. \"This Xiwei soil is prime black mud washed down from high mountains by the Wu River—rich enough to ooze oil. So long as you put your back into it, plant a single seed and it'll repay you with an entire trellis of long, sweet melons.\"\n\nAh-Shun carefully covered the seeds with soil and patted it firm with his palm. Looking up with his small face, he asked, \"Grandpa, that grand-aunt who married over from Changhua next to the school often says: 'Plant a single melon vine, and its tendrils wind through three counties.' What does that actually mean? No matter how fast a vine climbs, can it really reach all the way into Nantou?\"\n\nUncle Shuisheng couldn't help but burst into hearty laughter, the whistling wind escaping through the gap of his missing front tooth. Dropping his bamboo spade, he took Ah-Shun by the hand and led him out through the bamboo fence, heading toward the massive earthen-and-concrete embankment that sprawled along the edge of the village.\n\n\"Silly kid, that's no joke; it's the bitter reality your old grandpa has lived through every single day of his life.\" Uncle Shuisheng pointed up at the grey earthen rampart that rose two stories high before them, sprawling across the plains like a slumbering dragon. \"Look here. Our Xiwei Village is wedged squarely between the Wu River to the north and the Maoluo River to the south. These two mighty rivers act like a pair of shears, slicing us, Taichung, Changhua, and Nantou into a patchwork of tattered rags.\"\n\n\"Look at that drainage ditch,\" Uncle Shuisheng pointed to a shallow watercourse just outside the courtyard wall. \"This side of the ditch is Wuri Township, Taichung County; take one step across and you're in Fenyuan Township, Changhua County; follow this dike less than a mile down, and the deed records it as Caotun Township, Nantou County. Back in the fourteenth year of Showa—when your grandpa was in his twenties—the Japanese, determined to keep downtown Wuri and Taichung City on the northern bank safe from flooding, built this towering Wu River Dike by force.\"\n\nAt this point, the smile on Uncle Shuisheng's face gradually faded. A complicated, bitter sorrow surfaced in the gaze he cast upon the great levee.\n\n\"Once the levee was finished, the high officials and townspeople on the northern bank could sleep easy on their pillows; but this dike threw our Xiwei Village mercilessly outside the walls. Whenever the summer floods arrived, the mountain torrents rolling down from Mount Hehuan couldn't drain out—they poured straight into our little 'washbasin.' We hold Taichung household registrations and belong to Changhua's daily living circle, but once the waters rise, we become abandoned, wandering ghosts that no one looks after. That's why they call us an 'island,' the 'Penghu of Taichung'!\"\n\nAh-Shun listened only half-comprehending, but he remembered vividly: during that monstrous rainstorm last summer, roaring yellow waters had submerged the great banyan tree at the village entrance overnight. The despairing bray of his family's yellow ox as it was swept away by the raging current still woke him up in cold sweats in the dead of night. From that day on, whenever he beheld a deep, churning expanse of rolling water, his legs turned to jelly against his will.\n\n\"Uncle Shuisheng! Ah-Shun! Come quick—an official notice from the township office has arrived!\"\n\nA booming shout pierced the morning fog. The village warden, dripping with sweat, came rattling furiously down the muddy levee path on a battered iron bicycle. A roll of bright red paper was tied to his rack, and a string of crackling firecrackers hung from his handlebars.\n\n\"What glorious news warrants lighting firecrackers at the crack of dawn?\" Uncle Shuisheng hollered back.\n\n\"The Wuri Township Office just approved the official charter—he's been reappointed!\" The warden leapt off his bike, breathlessly wiping the mud from his face, his voice trembling with uncontainable joy. \"Ah-Rong—Lin Quanrong—has been officially reappointed as our Xiwei Village's public ferryman! The township office will subsidize his monthly wage as usual, and every single soul in our village crosses the river free of charge!\"\n\nUpon hearing the announcement, farmers bent over planting seedlings throughout the surrounding paddies straightened their backs one after another, erupting into heartfelt cheers. To this cut-off community, the appointed ferryman was far more than an occupation; he was the sole lifeline that connected several hundred souls to the outside world.\n\n\"Come on, Ah-Shun! Let's go down to the ferry dock with Grandpa to see Uncle Rong!\" Uncle Shuisheng seized Ah-Shun's hand, striding quickly toward the crossing on the southern bank of the Wu River.\n\nAs they traversed the floodway breach in the great dike and reached the sandbank, the morning fog happened to be cleaved in two by the rising sun. Golden-red sunlight angled across the broad river beach, illuminating more than a dozen colossal, emerald-sheened thorny bamboo stalks laid out in immaculate alignment upon the sand.\n\nBy the shore sheltered under bamboo groves, a middle-aged man of modest height, lean and sinewy like an ancient tree root, stood barefoot in the warm sand. He was dressed in a coarse cloth short shirt, faded from endless soaking in sweat and mud, his dark skin glowing like polished bronze, the veins along his forearms knotted like thick vines.\n\nThat was Lin Quanrong, whom young and old in the village respectfully addressed as \"Uncle Rong.\"\n\nAt this moment, a smoldering heap of dry rice straw burned before Uncle Rong. Amidst thick, aromatic white smoke, an ember of dark red charcoal breathed out scorching flames. Uncle Rong held the tail end of a massive thorny bamboo stalk, as thick as a soup bowl, his expression as solemn as a blacksmith forging a sacred blade.\n\n\"Ah-Rong, congratulations! The notice from the township office is here—everyone can finally let their hearts rest easy!\" Uncle Shuisheng greeted him with a smile.\n\nUncle Rong did not look up immediately. His eyes remained fixed upon the segment of bamboo pole sizzling as it was roasted over the fire. Beads of sweat as large as beans rolled from his brow, hitting the embers with faint, sharp hisses.\n\n\"Uncle Shuisheng, hold the congratulations for now,\" Uncle Rong's voice was low and gravelly, carrying the resonance of a man who had fought against the river's roar for a lifetime. \"Spring has broken, the snowmelt is descending, and the river broadens day by day. Last year's old raft took a battering from the boulders; it won't survive this summer's high water. If I don't take advantage of these sunny days to craft a new raft, what am I going to use to ferry the children to school when classes start?\"\n\nWith that, the muscles along Uncle Rong's arms bulged fiercely. He braced his shoulder against the midsection of the bamboo and bore down with all his might, bending the fire-softened bamboo tip downward.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, get over here!\" Uncle Rong turned his head abruptly. His gaze, sharp as an eagle's strike, landed upon Ah-Shun, who stood bewildered with his trouser cuffs rolled up high.\n\nStartled, Ah-Shun instinctively shrank behind his grandfather. He had always been somewhat intimidated by Uncle Rong's stern, unsmiling face, and he feared the damp, silt-scented gravel beach even more.\n\n\"Come here—grip this hemp rope and pin the bamboo base with your foot!\" Uncle Rong's tone brooked no refusal, yet a steady warmth gleamed in the depths of his eyes. \"You're a boy, eleven years old; it's time your bones hardened. Watch and learn—this is called 'fire-bending the bamboo tip.'\"\n\nUnder his grandfather's encouraging nod, Ah-Shun gritted his teeth and dashed forward. The moment his bare feet struck the sand, he felt the radiant heat of the charcoal fire. Following Uncle Rong's instructions, he threw all his weight into hugging the front of the thick stalk, planting both heels firmly against the wooden stake driven into the sandpit.\n\nWhile dousing the blistering bamboo nodes with a rag soaked in cold water, Uncle Rong spoke patiently to Ah-Shun: \"Thorny bamboo is the hardest, most resilient bamboo of all, but it also has the stubbornest temper. If you don't roast it thoroughly over the flames and work with its grain to bend it gradually into an upturned prow, the raging current will snap it clean in two.\"\n\nUnder Uncle Rong's steady, powerful leverage and the quenching splash of cold water, the rigid tip of the massive thorny bamboo miraculously set into a curve, arching upward like the chest of a waterbird about to spread its wings and crest the waves.\n\n\"Do you see that?\" Uncle Rong wiped a smudge of soot from his face and pointed to the curved bamboo tip. \"With this upturned prow, when you shove off into the water, the raft will glide atop the crests of the waves instead of plowing headlong into a whirlpool to be swallowed whole. People are the exact same way, Ah-Shun.\"\n\nUncle Rong reached out a large, callused hand as rough as sandpaper and patted Ah-Shun's slender shoulder gently. The hand felt heavy and warm, carrying the formidable strength of decades baked under the sun and tempered by river rapids.\n\n\"Your grandpa told me that ever since last year's big flood, you've been terrified of stepping near the riverbank—that even catching a glimpse of white water sets you shivering. Is that true or not?\" Uncle Rong looked straight into Ah-Shun's eyes.\n\nAh-Shun's face flushed crimson instantly. He lowered his head in shame, twisting the hem of his shirt with both hands, unable to utter a single word.\n\n\"Fearing the water is nothing to be ashamed of,\" Uncle Rong's voice gentled. He lifted his gaze toward the vast, grey river rolling relentlessly westward before them. \"The power of nature is thousands, tens of thousands of times greater than man. When facing this Wu River, anyone who doesn't know how to fear it has long since washed out to sea to feed the fish.\"\n\nAh-Shun looked up in surprise at the legendary ferryman.\n\n\"But you must remember: fear or no fear, your footing cannot falter,\" Uncle Rong pointed toward the faint silhouette of the Dadu Mountain range on the northern shore. \"This river traps us here, but it cannot sever our hearts. In a few days, school starts; no matter how scared you are, you will cross aboard Uncle's new raft to attend your classes. When we hit midstream, if terror grips you, fix your eyes on Mount Dadu. As long as the mountain stands firm, the boat will not capsize, and your spirit will not waver.\"\n\nAh-Shun looked in the direction Uncle Rong was pointing. In the distance, Mount Dadu drifted in and out of the early spring mist like a bluish-green rampart standing sentinel over the roaring northern bank.\n\nHe looked down at the fire-curved thorny bamboo beneath his feet, which would soon form the backbone of the new raft. Its outer green rind had been roasted into a burnished, deep yellow, still radiating a lingering warmth beneath his fingertips.\n\nAt that moment, within the heart of the eleven-year-old farm boy, amidst the deeply rooted dread of the great river, a tiny, unprecedented yearning quietly sprouted—the desire to one day grip that very Wave-Calming Pole."},{"id":3,"rawContent":"# 第二章：夏汛驚濤——破曉六點的「求學渡」\n\n進入農曆五月的梅雨汛期，原本溫馴流淌的烏溪，像是一頭被連日暴雨徹底激怒的遠古巨獸。\n\n連綿的大雨在中央山脈傾盆傾瀉，順著陡峭的山勢狂奔而下。短短三天之內，南岸原本露出大片沙石與雜草的寬闊河灘全被吞噬殆盡，水面猛烈暴漲了三公尺以上，原本清淺平緩的溪流，變成了一條寬達五百多公尺、奔騰咆哮的黃泥濁浪。\n\n破曉清晨五點二十分，天色依然是一片沉甸甸的鉛灰色，空氣中瀰漫著濃重的泥漿腥味與濕冷的草木氣息。\n\n十一歲的李順安站在渡口堤岸下方的爛泥地裡，身上套著一件用廢棄化肥塑膠袋改裝成的簡易雨衣，雙手死死攥著藍色布書包的背帶。書包裡裝著幾本泛黃的國語、算術課本，為了防止被水打濕，母親昨晚特地用兩層厚油紙把它們嚴嚴實實地包裹好。\n\n在阿順身旁，陸陸續續聚集了十幾個來自溪尾各個角落的學童。有的孩子赤著腳，手裡拎著一雙用麻繩綁在一起的膠鞋；有的年紀只有八、九歲，縮在哥哥姊姊身後瑟瑟發抖。\n\n溪尾國小雖然已經獨立設校，但因位處孤島偏鄉，師資與教室極為匱乏，全校幾十個孩子只能混齡擠在一塊兒。村裡許多一心盼著孩子將來能考上初中的父母，咬緊牙根，紛紛將孩子送到對岸師資完備、規模宏大的母校——喀哩國小寄讀。\n\n這趟橫渡風浪的早班船，村裡人都叫它「求學渡」。\n\n「阿順，你臉色怎麼白成那樣？是不是又怕得想尿褲子啦？」同班的頑皮男孩阿泰戳了戳阿順的肋骨，發出一陣嘻嘻哈哈的嘲笑，「你看那浪頭，比牛背還高！等一下竹筏要是打個滾，你那點小膽子非被沖去臺灣海峽餵鯊魚不可！」\n\n阿順咬緊下唇沒有吭聲，但他藏在衣袖裡的雙手正不由自主地劇烈顫抖。\n\n眼前的烏溪哪裡像是一條河？分明是一條翻滾奔馳的泥漿巨龍。夾帶著樹枝、草根，甚至整棵被連根拔起的苦楝樹殘枝的大浪，在江心轟然拍擊，激起半人高的泥黃色飛沫，發出震耳欲聾的低沉轟鳴聲。只要看上一眼，阿順胃裡就一陣翻江倒海，去年黃牛被洪水捲走溺斃的慘烈畫面再次浮現在腦海中。\n\n「嘟——嘟——！」\n\n一聲短促清亮、極具穿透力的竹哨聲，穿透了狂風與水浪的咆哮，在河灘上驟然響起。\n\n原本喧鬧的孩子們瞬間安靜下來。\n\n只見在波濤起伏的岸邊迴水處，一艘用十二根大刺竹紮成的新竹筏正隨著浪頭上下顛簸。阿榮伯赤著一雙青筋暴起的大腳，穩穩釘在濕滑的竹排正中央。他頭戴一頂油亮厚實的大斗笠，身上裹著一件用棕櫚皮編織的古老蓑衣，雨水順著蓑衣的毛糙尖端不斷淌落。\n\n那一根長達五公尺的大刺竹定波篙，正牢牢橫握在他的臂彎裡。\n\n「大的站兩邊，小的蹲中間！包袱書包全抱在胸口，莫要亂動！」阿榮伯的嗓門宏亮如銅鐘，沉著地下達指令，「一個個慢慢走，踩著竹節凸起的地方，別踩空滑進水裡！」\n\n孩子們像是一群受訓有素的小鴨子，在大孩子的帶領下，小心翼翼地踩著跳板走上竹筏。\n\n輪到阿順時，他的右腳剛踏上微凸的竹身，整座竹筏猛地被迎面撲來的浪頭往上一頂，隨後急速下墜！阿順嚇得發出一聲短促的驚呼，膝蓋一軟，整個人向前跌跪在竹筏上，雙手瘋狂地抓緊身旁的竹竿，指甲泛白，渾身僵硬得像一塊石頭。\n\n阿泰在後面嗤笑了一聲，但阿榮伯冰冷的目光立刻掃了過去，阿泰嚇得趕緊縮回脖子。\n\n阿榮伯大步跨上前，一把拎起阿順的衣領，將他拉了起來，粗糙寬大的手掌在阿順冰涼的後頸上用力捏了一把。\n\n「站直！雙腳與肩同寬，膝蓋微微彎曲，像在泥巴地裡插秧一樣！」阿榮伯的聲音很沉，卻有種能穿透風浪的奇異力量，「河水晃，你就跟著它晃。你的身子若跟竹子一樣懂得彎，浪就掀不倒你。」\n\n阿順死死咬住牙關，按照阿榮伯的口訣，緩緩將兩隻赤腳踩在竹排的接縫與繩結處，努力讓沉重的重心下沉。\n\n「解纜！起篙！」\n\n阿榮伯單臂一揮，將繫在岸邊苦楝樹上的粗麻繩解開甩上船頭。隨後，他雙臂猛力一推，手中的定波篙在岸邊堅硬的卵石層上發出一聲刺耳的摩擦聲，整座滿載著十幾個孩子與希望的竹筏，頓時如同一片輕盈的竹葉，順著迴流滑入了狂暴的江面。\n\n剛離岸二十公尺，竹筏立刻被主流那股凶猛狂躁的下衝力量狠狠扯住。\n\n「嘩啦——！」\n\n一道迎面撲來的濁浪狠狠砸在竹筏翹起的竹尾上，碎成一片冰冷的泥漿雨，澆了全船孩子一頭一臉。幾個低年級的女孩嚇得嗚嗚低哭起來，阿泰也閉上了嘴，臉色慘白地蹲在船中央，死死抱住腳下的竹捆。\n\n阿順站在阿榮伯身後半步的位置。他緊緊閉上眼睛，耳邊全是濁流撞擊竹管的咚咚巨響，整艘竹筏在水流中劇烈地上下拋擲、左右傾斜，彷彿下一秒就會散架解體。\n\n「阿順！睜開眼睛！」\n\n頭頂突然傳來阿榮伯如雷般的暴喝。\n\n阿順渾身一顫，被迫睜開了被泥水糊住的雙眼。\n\n「看哪裡？我叫你看大肚山！」阿榮伯的雙腳如同生了根般死死抓在濕滑的竹皮上，任憑狂風暴雨將他的蓑衣吹得獵獵作響，他的脊梁依然挺得筆直如松，「你看腳下的浪，除了泥巴跟漩渦，你能看出條生路來嗎？抬頭！把下巴抬起來！」\n\n阿順顫巍巍地抬起頭。\n\n穿過漫天飛舞的雨絲與翻滾的濁浪，北岸那座蒼鬱平緩的大肚山脈，在暴風雨的灰色天幕下巍然屹立，一動不動。\n\n奇妙的是，當目光牢牢鎖定在那座不動如山的山頭時，阿順原本天旋地轉的眩暈感竟然奇蹟般地減輕了，耳邊喧囂混亂的巨響似乎也拉開了距離。\n\n「看準了沒有？山不動，你就知道河水到底在把咱們往哪裡推！」\n\n阿榮伯一邊大喊，一邊握住定波篙在深達數公尺的激流中飛快地探底。竹篙入水，發出「嗡」的一聲震顫，阿榮伯的身體順勢向左傾斜，借著激流撞擊竹篙的反作用力，巧妙地將竹筏從一股巨大的下吸旋渦邊緣滑了過去！\n\n這不是在與大自然蠻幹，而是在大河的狂暴脈搏中，尋找那一線順水推舟的微小契機。這就是阿榮伯三十年來積累的絕技——「借水行舟」。\n\n然而，就在竹筏行駛至江心最凶險的深水槽時，變故陡生！\n\n從上游幾百公尺遠的翻滾濁浪中，一截帶著枝葉、足足有合抱粗細的巨大泡水斷木，正以驚人的速度順流直下，如同一根攻城巨木般朝著竹筏的側腰狠狠撞來！\n\n「阿伯！小心木頭！」蹲在中間的阿泰發出變調的尖叫。\n\n此時竹筏正處於急流中段，若強行往兩側避讓，整艘筏子必定會被主流橫向掀翻；若是不避，那根數百斤重的沉木撞上脆弱的竹管，竹排瞬間就會斷裂散架！\n\n在全船孩子的尖叫聲中，阿榮伯眼神驟然冷冽如寒刃。\n\n「阿順！過來，握住篙頭！替我撐半口氣！」\n\n阿榮伯根本不給阿順猶豫的機會，一隻溫暖有力的大手閃電般抓起阿順冰涼的雙手，將那根沉重無比、正在激流中瘋狂震顫的大刺竹定波篙狠狠塞進了他的掌心！\n\n竹篙上傳來的巨大扭力，差點把瘦弱的阿順直接甩進江裡。冰冷的河水順著竹竿向上倒灌，激流的力量彷彿十幾匹野馬在掌心狂奔撕扯。阿順的掌心瞬間被粗糙的竹皮擦破，火辣辣的劇痛傳來。\n\n「不要放！雙腳生根！看著大肚山！」阿榮伯在阿順耳邊怒吼。\n\n那一刻，阿順腦海中閃過家裡那頭被沖走哀鳴的黃牛，閃過母親用油紙包裹好的課本，閃過身後十幾個同村孩子驚恐的眼睛。\n\n「啊——！」\n\n十一歲的少年爆發出一聲帶著哭腔的嘶吼。他沒有鬆手，而是拼盡全身的力氣，將整個身體的重量死死壓在定波篙上，雙腳腳趾深深摳進竹排的縫隙中，硬生生頂住了那一股險些將竹筏帶偏的暗流！\n\n就在阿順頂住這千鈞一髮的兩秒鐘內，阿榮伯騰出了雙手。他抓起船尾備用的短竹橈，整個人躍至竹筏邊緣，肌肉如鋼鐵般鼓起，朝著奔騰而來的巨木狠狠一撥！\n\n「砰！」\n\n一聲沉悶的撞擊聲伴隨著飛濺的浪花爆開。巨大的斷木擦著竹筏翹起的竹尾邊緣險險掠過，翻滾著衝向下游。\n\n竹筏劇烈搖晃了幾下，終於穩穩穿過了江心最凶險的急流區，順著北岸平緩的迴水灣，輕柔地滑向了北堤渡口的泥灘。\n\n「嚓——」\n\n竹筏底部擦過淺灘上的軟泥，穩穩停了下來。\n\n整個世界彷彿在這一瞬間安靜了下來，只剩下北岸竹林裡清脆的鳥鳴，以及孩子們劫後餘生的急促喘息聲。\n\n阿榮伯收回竹篙，看著依然維持著前撲姿勢、雙手死死抱著定波篙不放、渾身濕透發抖的阿順。老擺渡人嚴肅的嘴角第一次微微上揚，眼底泛起了一抹極深的欣慰與讚許。\n\n「好了，阿順，鬆手吧。咱們過來了。」阿榮伯輕聲說道。\n\n阿順這才如夢初醒。他緩緩鬆開僵硬發麻的手指，只見兩隻手掌心已經被磨破了皮，滲出淡淡的血絲，混著黃色的泥水。\n\n阿泰和其他孩子們看著阿順，眼神裡再也沒有了平日的嘲諷與取笑，取而代之的是一種發自內心的敬佩。\n\n阿榮伯彎下腰，用自己粗糙的大拇指揩掉阿順額頭上的泥漿，沉聲說道：「阿順，剛才要是沒有你頂住那兩秒，咱們這條竹筏過不來。你今天不是膽小鬼，你是全船人的護篙手。」\n\n淚水終於混合著雨水從阿順的眼眶中奪眶而出，但這一次，不是因為恐懼，而是因為某種在他骨子裡破土而出的尊嚴與力量。\n\n孩子們紛紛背起書包，踩著跳板奔向北堤的石階。晨光穿透雲層，將喀哩國小紅色的校舍遠遠照亮。\n\n阿順走在隊伍的最後面。登上堤頂前，他忍不住回過頭，望向茫茫江面上那一艘隨著微波輕輕起伏的竹筏，以及竹筏上那個手握定波篙、如雕像般佇立的老擺渡人。\n\n在那一刻，少年的心中暗暗立下了一個無比宏大的誓願：\n\n長大以後，他一定要學會世界上最強大的本領，在這條暴烈的烏溪之上，親手造一座沖不垮、淹不滅、誰也不用再冒著生命危險過江的大橋！","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 2: Summer Floods and Churning Waves—The 6:00 AM School Crossing\n\nWith the onset of the fifth lunar month's plum-rain flood season, the Wu River—ordinarily flowing with meek grace—turned into an ancient behemoth completely enraged by days of torrential storms.\n\nRelentless downpours cascaded across the Central Mountain Range, stampeding wildly down steep slopes. Within a brief span of three days, the broad gravel bars on the southern bank, once carpeted with wild reeds and stones, were devoured whole. The water level surged upward by more than three meters, transforming what had been a gentle, shallow brook into a furious expanse of roiling yellow mud over five hundred meters wide.\n\nAt 5:20 on a daybreak morning, the sky hung like a slab of heavy, leaden grey, the air saturated with the rank mineral stench of clay and the sodden chill of crushed vegetation.\n\nEleven-year-old Li Shun'an stood in the sticky mire below the ferry embankment, wrapped in a makeshift raincoat fashioned from an old plastic fertilizer sack, his hands clutching the straps of his blue cloth school satchel for dear life. Inside rested his dog-eared Mandarin and arithmetic textbooks, which his mother had wrapped meticulously in two layers of oiled paper the night before to keep the river spray from ruining them.\n\nBeside Ah-Shun, a dozen schoolchildren hailing from every corner of Xiwei Village gradually assembled. Some stood barefoot, carrying their rubber shoes tied together with hemp twine; others, barely eight or nine years old, shrank shivering behind their older siblings.\n\nAlthough Xiwei Elementary School had become an independent institution, its isolated location meant severe shortages of teachers and classrooms, forcing a few dozen pupils of all ages to cram into multi-grade classrooms. Many village parents, desperately hoping their children might one day pass the entrance exams for junior high, gritted their teeth and sent their youngsters across the river to board or enroll at their well-equipped parent school—Kali Elementary School.\n\nThis dawn voyage across wind and water was what the villagers called the \"School Crossing.\"\n\n\"Hey Ah-Shun, why is your face so ghostly pale? Are you so terrified you're about to pee your pants again?\" A-Tai, a mischievous classmate, poked Ah-Shun in the ribs, cackling gleefully. \"Look at those swells—they're taller than an ox's back! If the bamboo raft takes a roll, that flea-sized courage of yours will be swept straight into the Taiwan Strait to feed the sharks!\"\n\nAh-Shun bit his lower lip and said nothing, yet his hands hidden inside his damp sleeves trembled violently against his will.\n\nHow could the expanse before them be called a river? It was a stampeding dragon of yellow mud. Monstrous waves laden with driftwood, weed clumps, and even torn boughs from uprooted chinaberry trees collided violently in midstream, churning up half-man-high froth of yellow foam and emitting a deafening, gut-shaking rumble. With a single glance, Ah-Shun's stomach heaved; that horrifying memory from last year—their yellow ox crying out in despair as it was dragged beneath the rapids—flashed once more before his eyes.\n\n\"Toot—toot—!\"\n\nA short, piercing note from a bamboo whistle cut through the howls of gale and water, sounding abruptly across the river beach.\n\nThe unruly clamor of the children went dead silent in an instant.\n\nThere, in the swirling backwater near the shore, a new raft lashed from twelve giant thorny bamboo trunks bobbed rhythmically against the swells. Uncle Rong stood barefoot, his veined, broad feet pinned like iron spikes to the center of the slick bamboo deck. He wore a broad, oiled conical hat and an ancient cape woven from coarse palm fiber, water trickling continuously from its frayed bristles.\n\nCradled firmly within the crook of his arm was that very five-meter-long thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole.\n\n\"The bigger ones stand along the sides, the little ones squat in the middle! Hold your bags tight against your chests—no moving about!\" Uncle Rong's voice boomed like a bronze bell, barking instructions with unshakeable calm. \"Board one by one. Plant your feet on the raised bamboo nodes; don't step into the gaps and slip into the river!\"\n\nLike disciplined ducklings led by the older children, the group carefully stepped across the gangplank onto the raft.\n\nWhen it was Ah-Shun's turn, his right foot barely grazed the ribbed bamboo before an incoming surge pitched the raft sharply upward, only to drop it plummeting the next second! Ah-Shun let out a choked gasp as his knees gave way. He fell forward onto the deck, his hands clawing wildly at the bamboo stalks beside him, his knuckles stark white, his entire frame frozen stiff as granite.\n\nA-Tai snickered from behind, but Uncle Rong's icy glare snapped his way, causing the prankster to yank his neck into his collar immediately.\n\nUncle Rong strode forward, hoisted Ah-Shun up by his collar with a single hand, and gave the boy's cold nape a firm, grounding squeeze.\n\n\"Stand tall! Feet shoulder-width apart, knees slightly flexed, just like planting rice seedlings in deep mud!\" Uncle Rong's tone was heavy, yet possessed a mysterious power that cut through the tempest. \"When the river sways, you sway with it. If your body learns to bend like bamboo, the waves will never knock you down.\"\n\nClenching his teeth, Ah-Shun followed Uncle Rong's mantra, shifting his bare feet onto the reinforced seams and rope lashes, willing his center of gravity to sink deep.\n\n\"Cast off! Raise the pole!\"\n\nWith a sweep of his arm, Uncle Rong unhitched the thick hemp hawser from an old chinaberry tree ashore and hauled it onto the bow. Then, driving his arms forward with immense leverage, the Wave-Calming Pole screeched against the submerged riverbed gravel, and the entire raft—laden with more than a dozen children and their hopes—glided smoothly like a slender willow leaf, carried by the eddy out into the raging main current.\n\nA mere twenty meters from the bank, the raft was seized violently by the savage, downward pull of the main channel.\n\n\"Crash—!\"\n\nA head-on wave battered the upturned bamboo prow, shattering into a spray of icy mud that rained down upon the children's heads and faces. Several lower-grade girls began to sob softly, while A-Tai fell dead silent, crouching pale-faced in the raft's belly, hugging the bundled bamboo underfoot.\n\nAh-Shun stood half a step behind Uncle Rong. He shut his eyes tightly, hearing only the hollow, booming thuds of brown rapids slamming against bamboo hulls. The raft was pitched violently upward and downward, banking side to side as though it might rip itself apart at any second.\n\n\"Ah-Shun! Open your eyes!\"\n\nA thunderous roar from Uncle Rong shattered the storm above.\n\nTrembling, Ah-Shun was forced to open his eyes, blurred with grit and mud.\n\n\"Where are you looking? I told you to look at Mount Dadu!\" Uncle Rong's feet were welded to the slippery bamboo skin. Though the gusts whipped his palm cape wildly, his spine remained as upright as an ancient pine. \"You stare at the foam beneath your feet—apart from sludge and whirlpools, can you find a shred of a path out? Look up! Lift your chin!\"\n\nShakily, Ah-Shun tilted his head upward.\n\nPiercing through the curtains of driving rain and tumbling waves, the dark green, gentle ridge of Mount Dadu stood motionless and monumental beneath the stormy canopy of the northern bank.\n\nMiraculously, the moment his eyes locked onto that unyielding mountain ridge, Ah-Shun's dizzying vertigo began to fade, and the chaotic din around him seemed to pull back into the distance.\n\n\"Do you see it clearly now? If the mountain doesn't move, you will know exactly where this river is trying to drag us!\"\n\nUncle Rong shouted as he drove the Wave-Calming Pole deep into the torrential channel, sounding the riverbed several meters below. The pole entered the water with a high-pitched hum of tension. Uncle Rong leaned his weight to the left, utilizing the violent rebound of water striking bamboo to skillfully slip the raft along the edge of a massive, gaping whirlpool!\n\nThis was no reckless battle against nature; it was finding that sliver of momentum within the river's wild pulse to hitch a ride on its current. This was Uncle Rong's master technique honed over thirty years—\"Harnessing the Current to Steer the Craft.\"\n\nHowever, just as the raft sliced into the most perilous midstream trench, disaster struck without warning!\n\nSeveral hundred meters upstream within the roiling brown waves, a waterlogged tree trunk as thick as a man's embrace came hurtling down the rapids at staggering speed, charging like a battering ram straight toward the raft's exposed flank!\n\n\"Uncle! Watch out for the log!\" crouched in the middle, A-Tai shrieked in terror.\n\nThe raft was pinned squarely within the main chute; if they swung broadside to dodge, the current would roll them upside down in a heartbeat. If they held their course, several hundred pounds of waterlogged timber smashing into fragile bamboo would splinter the raft instantly!\n\nAmid the frantic screams of the children, Uncle Rong's eyes turned sharp as cold steel.\n\n\"Ah-Shun! Come here—take the head of the pole! Hold it for half a breath!\"\n\nWithout giving Ah-Shun a moment to hesitate, Uncle Rong's warm, iron-strong hand clamped onto the boy's chilled palms, thrusting the colossal, violently shuddering thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole straight into his grip!\n\nThe sheer torque transmitted through the bamboo nearly flung the scrawny boy headlong into the depths. Freezing water surged up the pole, the power of the torrent pulling against his small palms like a herd of wild horses. The rough bamboo rind scraped his skin raw in an instant, sending a searing flash of pain through his arms.\n\n\"Don't let go! Root your feet! Look at Mount Dadu!\" Uncle Rong bellowed beside his ear.\n\nIn that split second, the image of that drowning ox flashed through Ah-Shun's mind, along with the memory of his mother's paper-wrapped books, and the terrified eyes of a dozen village children behind him.\n\n\"Ahhh—!\"\n\nThe eleven-year-old boy erupted in a tearful roar. He did not let go. Throwing the entire weight of his body down onto the Wave-Calming Pole, his bare toes dug fiercely into the bamboo seams, holding out against the undertow that threatened to rip them sideways!\n\nIn those two agonizing seconds bought by Ah-Shun, Uncle Rong freed his hands. Grabbing the spare wooden paddle from the stern, the veteran sprang to the edge of the raft, his muscles bunching like iron, and shoved fiercely against the surging timber!\n\n\"Thump!\"\n\nA dull crash exploded with a burst of spray. The colossal log skimmed the curved bamboo tail by inches, tumbling harmlessly into the downstream rapids.\n\nThe raft rocked violently a few times before leveling out, finally cutting across the most murderous midstream chute and gliding softly into the gentle backwaters of the northern dike's mudflat.\n\n\"Scrape—\"\n\nThe bamboo belly slid over the soft silt of the shallows, coming to a steady halt.\n\nThe entire world seemed to fall quiet in an instant, leaving only the crisp chirping of birds in the northern bamboo grove and the ragged gasps of the children who had cheated death.\n\nUncle Rong retrieved the pole and looked at Ah-Shun, who remained lunged forward, soaked to the bone and shivering, his hands still clutching the Wave-Calming Pole in a death grip. For the first time, the stern corners of the old ferryman's mouth curved upward into an expression of profound pride and approval.\n\n\"All right, Ah-Shun. You can let go now. We made it across,\" Uncle Rong said softly.\n\nOnly then did Ah-Shun awaken as if from a dream. Slowly prying his stiff, numb fingers apart, he saw the raw abrasions on both palms, faint traces of blood mingling with yellow river silt.\n\nA-Tai and the other children stared at Ah-Shun, their eyes devoid of their usual mockery, replaced by genuine, heartfelt awe.\n\nUncle Rong bent down, using his rough thumb to wipe the mud from Ah-Shun's forehead, his voice deep and solemn: \"Ah-Shun, if you hadn't held that pole for those two seconds, our raft wouldn't have made it. You weren't a coward today—you were the guardian of this boat.\"\n\nTears mingled with rainwater finally burst from Ah-Shun's eyes, but this time, it was not out of terror. It was from a newfound dignity and strength taking root deep within his bones.\n\nOne by one, the children hoisted their satchels and darted across the gangplank onto the stone steps of the northern dike. Morning sunlight pierced through the storm clouds, illuminating the red schoolhouse of Kali Elementary School in the distance.\n\nAh-Shun walked at the very rear of the group. Before cresting the levee, he couldn't help but look back across the vast river toward the slender raft bobbing gently in the shallows, and the old ferryman standing motionless like a monument with his Wave-Calming Pole.\n\nAt that exact moment, a resolute vow was silently forged within the young boy's heart:\n\nWhen he grew up, he would master the greatest knowledge in the world to build an unshakeable, unfloodable bridge across this wild Wu River with his own two hands—so that no one would ever have to risk their lives to cross this water again!"},{"id":4,"rawContent":"# 第三章：秋穗生金——沙洲西瓜與黑土冠軍米\n\n過了白露，酷烈躁動的夏汛終於緩緩退去。\n\n烏溪的水位降了兩米有餘，原本濁浪滔天的江面重歸寧靜，水色由渾濁的黃褐漸漸沉澱為一種深邃的青灰。\n\n退去的洪水在南岸留下了大自然最慷慨的饋贈——一層足有半尺厚、細膩得宛如脂膏般的黑色沖積土，覆蓋了整片河灘與鄰近的水田。那是在中央山脈深處蘊蓄了千萬年的頁岩與板岩風化物，吸飽了天地靈氣，富含著各類礦物質與有機腐殖質。\n\n放眼望去，整個溪尾寮的平原被染成了一片無邊無際的璀璨金黃。\n\n沉甸甸的稻穗垂下了頭，每一株稻稈都結實得像小姑娘編織的粗辮子。微風吹過，稻浪層層疊疊翻湧起伏，空氣中瀰漫著一股讓人聞之心醉的甜香，那是一種混雜著乾燥日照、新鮮米糠與純淨泥土的甘美氣味。\n\n十二歲的李順安赤著雙腳踩在濕潤微涼的黑土田埂上，手裡握著一把剛磨得雪亮的月牙小鐮刀。\n\n「阿順，割稻子要用巧勁，手腕往下壓，刀口斜斜地貼著根部拉！」外公水生伯彎著腰，手中鐮刀如飛，發出清脆悅耳的「唰唰」聲，一叢叢飽滿的稻穗應聲倒下，整整齊齊地碼在田埂邊。\n\n水生伯直起腰，隨手揪下幾顆金黃的稻穀，用粗糙的大拇指在掌心用力一搓，外層的穀殼頓時脫落，露出了晶瑩剔透、宛如白玉般的米粒。水生伯把生米直接扔進嘴裡細細咀嚼，臉上洋溢著無比自豪的笑容。\n\n「香！真香！」水生伯笑瞇瞇地瞇起眼睛，「阿順你嚼嚼看，咱們溪尾這黑土種出來的米，帶著一股淡淡的芋頭甜味。日後送到公糧局去評比，哪一家農會的穀子能比得上咱們烏溪沖出來的糧倉？」\n\n阿順學著阿公的樣子嚼了幾粒生米，舌尖果然泛起一股清甜細膩的回甘。他望著滿田滿谷的金色波浪，幾個月前在暴風雨中渡江求學的驚恐與疲憊，似乎都在這片豐收的馨香中被洗滌得乾乾淨淨。\n\n「水生叔！阿順！西瓜熟透啦，再不採收就要被鳥雀啄爛囉！」\n\n遠處河灘上的沙洲方向，傳來了阿榮伯宏亮的呼喊聲。\n\n阿順眼睛一亮，立刻放下鐮刀，跟著水生伯快步朝著烏溪南岸的開闊沙洲跑去。\n\n那是一片由粗砂、鵝卵石與退水後薄泥混合而成的貧瘠灘地，平日裡寸草不生，但在炎熱乾燥的秋季，卻成了栽種西瓜的絕佳寶地。白天，滾燙的鵝卵石吸收了烈日的曝曬；夜晚，冰涼的江風又讓沙地迅速降溫。劇烈的日夜溫差，把糖分毫不保留地逼進了瓜肉的最深處。\n\n只見在密密麻麻如綠色地毯般的瓜藤間，靜靜躺著一顆顆深綠條紋、足有小臉盆大小的滾圓大西瓜。\n\n阿榮伯正蹲在一顆碩大的西瓜旁，屈起手指輕輕在瓜皮上叩了叩。\n\n「咚、咚、咚！」\n\n空氣中傳來一陣極其清脆、甚至帶著微微震顫的沉悶回音，像是敲在一面緊繃的牛皮小鼓上。\n\n「熟得恰到好處，皮薄、肉沙、起沙糖！」阿榮伯滿意地點點頭，手起刀落，切斷了瓜藤，雙手將這顆足足有二十幾斤重的大西瓜抱了起來，「水生叔，今年這批沙洲瓜長得極好，運到對岸烏日市區的菜市場，保管半個時辰就被搶光！」\n\n「好是好，但這幾百顆大西瓜，還有田裡幾十麻袋的乾穀子，沒有大橋，該怎麼運過江啊？」阿順看著堆積如山的瓜果與糧食，有些發愁地問道。\n\n「哈哈！傻小子，你忘了咱們渡口有什麼啦？」水生伯拍了拍阿順的後腦勺，指了指泊在岸邊的巨型竹筏。\n\n此時的竹筏已經不是夏天下載學童的輕巧模樣。為了應對秋收，阿榮伯在原本的十二根刺竹兩側，又各加捆了四根粗壯的副竹，使得整艘竹筏的寬度達到了將近三公尺，載重量提升了一倍以上。\n\n「阿順，今天你來當阿伯的『壓艙副手』！」阿榮伯招了招手，「咱們把糧食和西瓜運上筏，這叫『水上運糧船』！」\n\n阿順興奮地應了一聲，捲起袖子加入搬運的隊伍。\n\n大人們排成一條長龍，手遞手地將沉甸甸的西瓜送上竹筏。西瓜被整整齊齊地碼放在竹排的中央凹槽處，四周用軟稻草嚴密包裹，防止碰撞滾動；竹筏的兩側則壓上一袋袋裝滿優質稻穀的麻布袋。\n\n整整碼了八十顆大西瓜、六大袋稻穀，整艘竹筏的吃水變得極深，滾圓的竹管幾乎有一大半浸沒在冰涼的江水裡，河水順著竹縫輕輕漫過腳背。\n\n「解纜！出港！」\n\n隨著阿榮伯一聲悠長的號子，阿順動作敏捷地解開纜繩，隨後抓起一根稍短的柏木划槳，穩穩立在船尾。\n\n阿榮伯站在船頭，手中的大刺竹定波篙輕輕一點河底卵石，載滿了全村一整年心血結晶的運糧船，便緩緩破開如鏡的水面，朝著對岸滑行而去。\n\n此時正值黃昏時分，夕陽如同一團融化了的金子，將整條平緩流淌的烏溪染成了燦爛的橘紅色。江風不再像夏天那樣兇狂冰冷，而是帶著稻田的芬芳與草木的微溫，輕柔地拂過每個人的臉頰。\n\n阿順握著木槳，眼睛不再像從前那樣充滿恐懼與慌亂。他學會了感知腳下竹筏的微小晃動，每當水流稍微偏斜，他的腳掌便配合著阿榮伯的竹篙自然調整重心，整個人如同一棵扎根在竹排上的柔韌小樹。\n\n「阿順，水性跟人性是一樣的。」阿榮伯一邊悠閒地撐著長篙，一邊回過頭看著漸漸成熟穩健的少年，語氣裡帶著一種歷經滄桑後的通透，「夏天大水暴漲的時候，你得敬它、順它，不能跟它硬拼；秋天水溫退去了，它又把上游最好的土、最甜的水留給了咱們。這大河雖然險，但要是沒有它，就沒有咱們溪尾這滿倉的香米，沒有這甜透心脾的沙洲瓜。」\n\n水生伯盤腿坐在糧袋旁，從腰間抽出一柄雪亮的小折刀，順手將身旁一顆翠綠的西瓜「喀嚓」一聲剖成兩半。\n\n鮮紅誘人的瓜瓤頓時呈現在眼前，晶瑩剔透的黑色瓜籽鑲嵌在起沙的果肉間，一股清冽甘甜的瓜香瞬間在江面上瀰漫開來。\n\n「來！一人一半，嘗嘗咱們大河沖出來的甜水！」水生伯切下厚厚的一大瓣瓜，先遞給了阿榮伯，又遞給了阿順。\n\n阿順咬下一大口瓜肉。\n\n冰涼甘甜的汁水頓時在口腔中四溢開來，那種沙糯甜脆的口感，洗去了連日割稻挑擔所有的疲勞，一直甜到了心坎裡。\n\n「甜！比糖水還要甜！」阿順忍不住開心地喊道。\n\n三個世代的男人坐在夕陽映照的運糧竹筏上，一邊大口吃著甜美的沙洲西瓜，一邊隨波緩緩前行。遠處北岸渡口已經聚集了許多等待接應的城鎮商販與推車，炊煙在對岸的村莊裊裊升起。\n\n「阿順啊，」水生伯擦了擦嘴角紅色的瓜汁，望著漸漸靠近的對岸，若有所思地說道，「你讀書聰明，算術比村裡誰都好。但你得記住，以後不管走到哪裡、不管讀了多高的學問，你的根是在這片黑泥巴裡長出來的。人不能忘本，就像這瓜藤，爬得再遠，根還是在自家院子裡。」\n\n阿順看著腳下被黑土養育的稻穀，看著手裡甘甜無比的西瓜，用力點了點頭：「阿公，我記住了。等我長大了，我要用這片土地教給我的東西，為咱們村子做大事。」\n\n運糧竹筏帶著滿船的金黃與甘甜，輕輕撞上了北岸的石灘，發出扎實而滿足的悶響。\n\n兩岸的歡聲笑語交織在秋夜的晚風中，這一刻，大河不再是阻隔生命的深淵，而是一條流淌著乳汁與蜜糖的金色動脈。","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 3: Golden Grains of Autumn—Sandbar Watermelons and Champion Black-Soil Rice\n\nPast the solar term of White Dew, the brutal and restless summer floods finally began their slow retreat.\n\nThe water level of the Wu River dropped by more than two meters, and the once-turbid expanse regained its composure, its color settling from a murky yellowish-brown into a profound slate-grey.\n\nThe receding waters left behind nature's most lavish gift along the southern shore—a layer of fine, rich alluvial black soil, nearly half a foot thick, as supple as soothing balm, blanketing the river beach and adjacent paddies. This was shale and slate weathered over millions of years in the deep heart of the Central Mountain Range, having absorbed the essence of heaven and earth, saturated with minerals and fertile organic humus.\n\nAs far as the eye could see, the plains of Xiwei Village were transformed into an ocean of boundless, resplendent gold.\n\nHeavy ears of rice bowed their heads, each stem as sturdy as a young girl's braided hair. Whenever a breeze swept through, billows of rice surged and tumbled in layered waves, filling the air with an intoxicating sweetness—a delicious fragrance blending dry sunlight, fresh rice bran, and pure earth.\n\nTwelve-year-old Li Shun'an stood barefoot on the cool, damp black ridge of the paddy, holding a small crescent sickle ground to a mirror shine.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, harvesting rice takes a deft wrist! Press downward and draw the blade diagonally against the base of the stalk!\" Uncle Shuisheng bent at the waist, his sickle flying through the grain with crisp, rhythmic swishes. Clusters of plump rice heads fell obediently at his feet, stacking into neat rows along the ridge.\n\nUncle Shuisheng straightened his spine and casually plucked a few golden grains, rubbing them briskly between his thick, callused thumb and palm. The outer husks flaked away, revealing crystalline, jade-like kernels. Popping the raw rice directly into his mouth to chew, his face beamed with immense pride.\n\n\"Aromatic! Utterly aromatic!\" Uncle Shuisheng narrowed his eyes into smiling slits. \"Chew a few, Ah-Shun. The rice grown in our Xiwei black soil carries a delicate hint of taro sweetness. When these grains are sent to the grain bureau for evaluation, whose association harvest could ever hold a candle to the granary flushed out by our Wu River?\"\n\nAh-Shun chewed a few raw grains just like his grandfather, and a clean, subtle sweetness immediately bloomed across his tongue. Looking out across the valleys of shimmering gold, the terror and exhaustion of crossing the river for school during the summer storms seemed entirely washed away by this bountiful fragrance.\n\n\"Uncle Shuisheng! Ah-Shun! The watermelons are dead-ripe—if we don't harvest them today, the birds will peck them to bits!\"\n\nFrom the direction of the sandbars on the river beach came Uncle Rong's booming shout.\n\nAh-Shun's eyes lit up. Setting down his sickle, he dashed alongside his grandfather toward the sprawling sandbar on the southern bank of the river.\n\nThis was a barren spit formed by coarse grit, river gravel, and thin dried silt—ordinarily unable to sustain a blade of grass. Yet in the hot, parched autumn months, it became prime real estate for growing watermelons. By day, the scalding river stones absorbed the blazing sun; by night, the chilled river wind cooled the ground with haste. This sharp diurnal temperature swing forced every drop of natural sugar into the deepest core of the melon flesh.\n\nThere, nestled amidst a dense, emerald carpet of tangled vines, rested massive round melons with dark green stripes, each as large as a washbasin.\n\nUncle Rong crouched beside an enormous melon, rapping his knuckles lightly against its rind.\n\n\"Thump, thump, thump!\"\n\nA remarkably crisp, deep vibration echoed through the air, reminiscent of striking a taut calfskin drum.\n\n\"Ripened to perfection—paper-thin rind, grainy crimson flesh, and crystallizing sugar!\" Uncle Rong nodded with satisfaction. With a swift flick of his knife, he severed the stem and hoisted the watermelon, easily weighing over twenty catties, into his arms. \"Uncle Shuisheng, this batch of sandbar melons is exceptional. Ferry them to the morning market in downtown Wuri, and they'll be snapped up in half an hour flat!\"\n\n\"Magnificent indeed, but with several hundred melons and dozens of burlap sacks of dry grain from the fields, how are we going to haul it all across without a bridge?\" Ah-Shun asked, eyeing the mountainous heaps of fruit and produce with furrowed brows.\n\n\"Haha! Silly boy, did you forget what we keep at our ferry dock?\" Uncle Shuisheng patted the back of Ah-Shun's head, pointing toward the colossal bamboo raft moored along the bank.\n\nThis raft was no longer the nimble craft that carried schoolchildren during the summer. To accommodate the autumn harvest, Uncle Rong had lashed four additional thick bamboo trunks to each flank of the original twelve, broadening the deck to nearly three meters and more than doubling its carrying capacity.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, today you'll be my 'ballast mate'!\" Uncle Rong beckoned. \"We're loading grain and melons onto the raft—we call this the 'Waterborne Grain Barge'!\"\n\nAh-Shun answered enthusiastically, rolling up his sleeves to join the line.\n\nThe grown-ups formed a human chain, passing the heavy melons hand to hand onto the deck. The watermelons were stowed neatly in the raft's central depression, cushioned with soft rice straw to prevent rolling and bruising. Along the outer flanks, they stacked heavy burlap sacks bulging with premium paddy rice.\n\nWith eighty large watermelons and six massive bags of grain aboard, the raft rode remarkably low in the water. More than half of each rounded bamboo stalk was submerged in the cool river, the current gently lapping across the tops of their bare feet.\n\n\"Cast off! Out to river!\"\n\nAt Uncle Rong's prolonged cadence call, Ah-Shun swiftly untied the mooring line and picked up a short cypress paddle, planting his feet firmly at the stern.\n\nUncle Rong stood proudly at the bow. With a gentle shove of the thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole against the gravelly riverbed, the grain barge—bearing an entire year's labor and dreams—parted the glassy surface and glided smoothly toward the opposite shore.\n\nIt was twilight. The setting sun, like a pot of molten gold, dyed the placidly rolling Wu River in hues of radiant orange. The river breeze was no longer the icy, feral gale of summer; it was mild and scented with ripening paddies, caressing their faces with gentle warmth.\n\nHolding his paddle, Ah-Shun's eyes were no longer clouded with dread. He had learned to read the subtlest shifts in the raft beneath his soles. Whenever the current tugged sideways, his feet worked instinctively with Uncle Rong's pole to counterbalance the tilt, his small frame bending like a supple willow rooted into the bamboo deck.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, the temper of water is just like the temper of a man,\" Uncle Rong said leisurely as he punted, glancing back at the increasingly poised young lad with the insight of a weathered sage. \"When the summer floods roar, you must respect it and yield to it; you cannot pick a fight with its fury. But when the autumn chill sets in, it leaves behind the richest soil and the sweetest water from the mountains. This great river is treacherous, yes, but without it, we would have neither this overflowing granary of aromatic rice nor these honey-sweet sandbar melons.\"\n\nUncle Shuisheng sat cross-legged beside the grain sacks. Pulling a polished pocketknife from his sash, he halved a green melon with a crisp \"crack.\"\n\nVibrant, crimson flesh lay revealed, glistening black seeds nestled within granular pulp, instantly releasing a cool, sweet perfume that drifted across the river.\n\n\"Here! Half for each of you—taste the sweet waters our great river washed down!\" Uncle Shuisheng sliced two thick wedges, handing the first to Uncle Rong and the second to Ah-Shun.\n\nAh-Shun took a massive bite.\n\nCool, sugary nectar exploded across his tongue. That sandy, crisp sweetness chased away every trace of weariness from days of harvesting and hauling, warming him to his very core.\n\n\"Sweet! Sweeter than sugarcane water!\" Ah-Shun shouted with pure delight.\n\nThree generations of men sat atop the grain barge under the evening glow, chewing sweet sandbar watermelon as they drifted across the water. On the northern bank, carts and town merchants had already gathered to receive the cargo, while spirals of cooking smoke drifted up from the villages beyond.\n\n\"Ah-Shun,\" Uncle Shuisheng wiped the red juice from the corner of his mouth, watching the nearing shore thoughtfully. \"You're sharp at school, and your arithmetic is better than anyone else's in the village. But you must never forget: no matter where you go in this world, or how much book-learning you master, your roots sprouted from this very black mud. A person must never forget where he came from—just like a melon vine; no matter how far its tendrils wander, its root remains right in our home soil.\"\n\nLooking down at the rice nurtured by the black silt, and then at the sweet watermelon in his hands, Ah-Shun nodded with fierce determination: \"Grandpa, I will remember. When I grow up, I will use what this land taught me to do great things for our home.\"\n\nCarrying a deck laden with gold and sweetness, the grain barge bumped softly against the stony beach of the northern shore with a solid, satisfying thud.\n\nLaughter and greetings from both banks mingled into the autumn evening breeze. In that golden hour, the great river was no longer an abyss separating human lives, but a shimmering artery flowing with nourishment and hope."},{"id":5,"rawContent":"# 第四章：冬寒裂骨——暗夜對岸的「生死火把」\n\n進入冬至，凜冽的東北季風如同一柄柄刮骨的冰刀，自海口方向逆流而上，呼嘯著席捲過烏溪兩岸蕭瑟的荒灘。\n\n這是一年中最寒冷的枯水期。烏溪寬廣的河床大半裸露在外，灰白色的巨大鵝卵石鋪滿了河灘，昔日奔騰咆哮的江水退縮成幾道深淺不一的灰色深水槽。\n\n為了節省村民每天搭筏渡江的體力與等待時間，阿榮伯帶著村裡的青壯年男人們，展開了一年一度浩大的工程——搭建百米「竹便橋」。\n\n阿榮伯指揮村民砍來上百根堅硬的長刺竹，將三根大竹紮成「人」字形的橋腳，深深打入深槽底部的亂石堆中，上面再橫鋪四道厚實的剖半毛竹作為橋面，最後用浸透麻油的粗鐵線牢牢絞緊。\n\n這座搖搖晃晃、僅容一人推著手推車勉強通行的簡易竹橋，橫跨在冰冷的河面上，宛如一條懸空浮動的白龍，連接著溪尾與對岸的同安厝。\n\n然而，大河的脾氣向來詭譎莫測。\n\n就在隆冬臘月一個寒流籠罩的黑夜，氣溫驟降到攝氏七度以下，漆黑的天幕突然撕裂，上游南投山區毫無預警地下起了一場極為罕見的狂暴冬雨。\n\n冰冷刺骨的山洪裹挾著深山的斷木與碎石，在深夜咆哮而至。\n\n「轟隆隆——！」\n\n沉悶的巨響在黑夜中震動大地。短短半個時辰，剛剛暴漲的泥流就像是一隻狂暴的巨掌，以摧枯拉朽之勢將那座耗費全村數週心血搭建的竹便橋硬生生撕成碎片，斷竹與鐵線在急流中哀鳴翻滾，眨眼間被捲向下游。\n\n凌晨兩點一刻，整個溪尾寮沉浸在死一般的寂靜與嚴寒中。\n\n「起火啦！對岸起火啦！」\n\n睡在三合院偏房的阿順被一陣急促的拍門聲與狗吠聲猛然驚醒。他裹上單薄的破棉襖衝出門外，只見外公水生伯正提著一盞防風煤油燈，神色凝重地望著烏溪北岸。\n\n穿過茫茫的黑夜與呼嘯的寒風，只見北堤廢棄的渡口石階上，一團橘紅色的煤油火把正在瘋狂地上下揮舞、畫著圓圈！\n\n緊接著，順著冰冷的江風，隱隱約約傳來一聲聲撕心裂肺、帶著哭腔的淒厲呼喊：\n\n「過溪喔——！人命關天啊——！阿榮叔！救命啊——！」\n\n那聲音在呼嘯的北風中斷斷續續，卻如同重錘一般狠狠砸在每個村民的心頭。\n\n「是對岸同安厝的林家二媳婦！」水生伯臉色慘白，聲音顫抖，「她懷胎九個月，聽說這兩天胎位不正一直在流血……北岸的公路被山石塌方堵死了，縣城的救護車根本進不來！他們要過溪！只有溪尾這邊有牛車可以走芬園送到彰化大醫院！」\n\n「可是……橋斷了啊！」阿順驚恐地大喊，「現在江水比墨汁還黑，水溫冰得能凍死人，誰能過得去？」\n\n「他能。」水生伯轉頭望向渡口，眼底閃爍著敬畏的光芒。\n\n當水生伯與阿順提著燈籠跌跌撞撞趕到渡口時，只見岸邊的老苦楝樹下，一艘被粗麻繩死死捆在一根刺竹樁上的大竹筏正在黑色濁流中劇烈顛簸。\n\n阿榮伯已經站在了竹筏上。他沒有穿蓑衣，只穿著一件單薄的粗布短褂，腰間繫著一根救命的草繩，嘴唇凍得發紫，但那雙鷹隼般的眼睛在煤油燈的映照下，冷冽得像兩柄出鞘的刺刀。\n\n「阿榮！不能下水啊！」趕來的保正一把扯住纜繩，老淚縱橫地大喊，「冬雨的山洪最陰毒，水裡全是暗木跟翻滾的冰塊，這黑燈瞎火的，一下去就是送死啊！」\n\n「送死也得去。」阿榮伯的聲音沒有半分顫抖，堅硬得宛如河底的磐石，「火把搖了三圈，那是催命的訊號。對岸是兩條人命，一個孕婦，一個還沒出世的娃兒。我林權榮接了公所這塊牌子，只要我還有一口氣在，這條河就不能斷了人家的生路！」\n\n說著，阿榮伯拔出腰間的小柴刀，猛地將繫在岸邊的最後一根粗纜繩劈斷！\n\n「阿榮伯！我跟你去！」\n\n一聲清脆而堅定的少年吶喊突然在寒風中炸響。\n\n只見十二歲的阿順一步跨上前，雙手死死抓起掛在支架上的防風馬燈，在所有人驚駭的目光中，毫不猶豫地縱身一躍，跳上了劇烈搖晃的竹筏！\n\n「阿順！你瘋啦！快上來！」水生伯嚇得魂飛魄散，在岸邊大喊。\n\n「阿公，北岸黑得看不見路！阿榮伯要兩隻手撐篙，沒人替他掌燈照暗礁！」阿順咬破了下嘴唇，鮮血的鹹味讓他無比清醒。他把馬燈高高舉過頭頂，雙腳深深扎在竹排的接縫裡，目光迎向錯愕的阿榮伯，「阿伯，你說過，我是全船的護篙手！我不怕了！」\n\n阿榮伯深深地看了眼前的少年一眼。他沒有再勸阻，只是重重從鼻腔裡吐出一口白色的熱氣：「好小子！骨頭硬了！把燈抓穩，照著前方三公尺的水線，看見黑沫子就喊！」\n\n「轟——！」\n\n大刺竹定波篙在岸邊亂石中奮力一撐，竹筏瞬間脫離了河灘，像是一支離弦的利箭，狠狠扎進了漆黑冰冷、深不見底的墨色大河之中！\n\n極致的嚴寒瞬間包裹了兩人。迎面撲來的江水不再是夏天的泥漿，而是冰冷刺骨、寒徹心扉的冰水，濺在臉上宛如無數細碎的鋼針在扎。\n\n四周是一片伸手不見五指的死寂，唯有阿順手中那盞搖曳的馬燈，在奔騰翻滾的黑水上投下一圈微弱而孤獨的黃光。\n\n「阿伯！左前方有暗木！」阿順大聲尖叫。\n\n借著微弱的燈光，阿榮伯看清了一截隱藏在黑水之下、隨波翻滾的尖銳斷竹樁。老擺渡人腰部發力，手中的定波篙在水底閃電般一點，竹筏擦著尖刺險險滑過。\n\n然而，就在竹筏行駛到深水槽最中央時，一場最致命的意外降臨了！\n\n一塊被山洪自上游沖下來的巨大滾石，在水底暗暗翻滾，正好狠狠砸在竹筏尾部的兩根副竹上！\n\n「喀嚓——！」\n\n伴隨著一聲刺耳欲聾的脆響，兩根粗壯的大刺竹當場被巨石砸得粉碎斷裂，緊繃的鉛線瞬間崩開，竹筏的一側猛地向下傾斜，冰冷漆黑的江水瘋狂地漫上竹排，整艘船開始向右劇烈打旋下沉！\n\n「竹排裂了！要翻船了！」阿順站立不穩，整個人摔在濕滑的竹皮上，但他依然拼死用懷抱護住了那盞微弱的馬燈。\n\n生死一線之間，阿榮伯做了一個讓阿順畢生難以忘懷的驚天舉動。\n\n老擺渡人將定波篙往阿順手裡一塞，大吼一聲：「阿順，抓緊竹篙，莫動！」\n\n話音未落，阿榮伯竟然毫不猶豫地翻身躍下竹筏，跳進了深達胸口、溫度接近冰點的滔滔黑水之中！\n\n「阿榮伯——！」阿順哭喊出聲。\n\n只見阿榮伯在冰冷刺骨的急流中宛如一尊鋼鐵鑄就的神祇。他用自己的雙肩與後背，死死頂住那幾根即將散架的斷裂竹管，任憑冰冷的濁浪一次又一次砸在他的胸膛與頭頂。他的雙腳在布滿青苔與尖石的河床暗礁上瘋狂地尋找著支撐點，每一步都在冰水裡踩出一道道血痕！\n\n「撐……撐住篙！阿順……借……借水力往北岸推啊！」阿榮伯在黑浪中吐出一口混雜著冰水的粗氣，脖子上的青筋暴突得如同虯結的老樹根，嘶啞的咆哮聲震碎了寒夜。\n\n阿順滿臉都是淚水與冰水，但他沒有哭泣的時間。\n\n十二歲的少年把心中的恐懼、嚴寒與顫抖全部化作了力量。他回想起無數次阿榮伯教他的姿勢，雙腿屈膝，雙臂將定波篙狠狠插進水底，配合著水底阿榮伯用血肉之軀頂住的推力，借著滔天大浪的推擠，一寸一寸、一尺一尺地將傾斜下沉的竹筏，硬生生頂出了死亡漩渦！\n\n五十米……三十米……十米！\n\n「抓住了！抓住纜繩啦！」\n\n對岸渡口傳來了男人們絕望後的狂喜呼喊。十幾個手持火把的壯丁紛紛跳入淺灘，七手八腳地抓住了竹筏前端甩過去的麻繩，拼盡全力將殘破的竹筏拉上了北岸的卵石灘！\n\n「阿榮叔！阿榮叔你怎麼樣了？！」\n\n眾人慌忙跳進水裡，將已經凍得全身發紫、僵硬如鐵的阿榮伯從冰水裡抬了出來。老人的肩膀與後背被斷竹劃得皮開肉綻，鮮血剛流出來就被冰水凍凝成了暗紅色，但他那雙深陷的眼睛依然死死盯著岸上的草棚。\n\n「產……產婦呢？」阿榮伯牙齒劇烈打顫，聲音微弱得幾不可聞。\n\n「在牛車上了！正在用被子裹著！阿榮叔，你們要是再晚來一刻鐘，母子倆就真的沒命了啊！」同安厝的漢子撲通一聲跪在泥地裡，對著阿榮伯與阿順重重磕了一個響頭。\n\n眾人合力將難產昏迷的少婦抬上了牛車，車伕猛甩皮鞭，牛車在泥濘的夜路上朝著芬園與彰化醫院瘋狂奔馳而去。\n\n黎明破曉時分，東方的天際泛起了一抹淡淡的魚肚白。\n\n狂暴的冬雨終於漸漸停歇，北岸的草棚裡點起了一堆溫暖的炭火。阿順坐在火堆旁，雙手緊緊捧著一碗滾燙的薑湯，看著身上裹了三層厚棉被、臉色終於由慘白轉為一絲紅潤的阿榮伯。\n\n就在這時，遠處通往彰化的公路方向，一輛自行車飛快地蹬了回來，車上的青年興奮得一路狂喊：\n\n「生啦！生啦！生了個大胖小子！母子平安！母子平安啊！」\n\n那一聲清脆的喜訊在初升的晨光中迴盪開來，瞬間驅散了整條烏溪所有的寒意。\n\n躺在棉被裡的阿榮伯緩緩睜開眼睛，聽著遠方傳來的喜訊，他那乾裂起皮的嘴角，終於浮現出了一抹極其欣慰、無比燦爛的微笑。\n\n阿順轉過頭，望向江心那座被大水沖毀、如今只剩下幾截殘破樁木的百米竹便橋，又看了看自己手掌心被粗竹磨破的新繭。\n\n少年在心底無比清晰地聽見了一種聲音——那是生命跨越深淵的啼哭，是人間守望相助的熱血，更是這條暴烈大河永遠也沖不垮的堅韌靈魂。","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 4: Bone-Splitting Winter Chill—The Torches of Life and Death Across the Dark Night\n\nEntering the Winter Solstice, the biting northeast monsoon swept upstream from the river mouth like bone-scraping daggers of ice, howling across the bleak, desolate flats flanking both banks of the Wu River.\n\nThis was the coldest dry season of the year. Most of the river's vast expanse lay exposed, giant chalk-grey riverbed boulders littering the shoals, while the once-tumultuous torrents dwindled into several dark, grey trenches of varying depth.\n\nTo spare the villagers the exhaustive labor and long waits of daily ferrying, Uncle Rong led the able-bodied men of the village in launching their grand annual undertaking—constructing the hundred-meter \"Temporary Bamboo Bridge.\"\n\nUncle Rong directed the villagers to fell over a hundred long, rigid thorny bamboo stalks. Lashed in threes to form sturdy tripod trestles shaped like the character \"人,\" these pilings were driven deep into the rubble at the bottom of the deep trenches. Four parallel runs of split giant moso bamboo were laid across the top to form the deck, secured tightly with heavy galvanized wire soaked in sesame oil.\n\nThis rickety, makeshift bamboo footpath—barely wide enough for a lone farmer to push a handcart—arched across the freezing water like a suspended white dragon, stitching Xiwei to Tong'ancuo on the northern bank.\n\nYet the river's temper had always been treacherous and unpredictable.\n\nOn a freezing night locked in the depths of the twelfth lunar month, with temperatures plunging below seven degrees Celsius, the pitch-black sky ripped open without warning. The Nantou highlands upstream were battered by a freak, violent winter deluge.\n\nFreezing mountain torrents, laden with shattered timber and boulders from deep ravines, came roaring downstream in the dead of night.\n\n\"Rumble—boom—!\"\n\nMuffled thunders shook the earth in the dark. Within a mere half-hour, the surging mudflow descended like a ravenous beast, mercilessly tearing the hundred-meter bamboo bridge—the fruit of weeks of community toil—into splinters. Fractured bamboo and severed wires rolled with anguished groans in the rapids, swept downstream in the blink of an eye.\n\nAt 2:15 in the morning, the whole of Xiwei Village lay submerged in dead silence and bitter cold.\n\n\"Fire! There's fire on the other side!\"\n\nSleeping in the side wing of the courtyard, Ah-Shun was startled awake by frantic pounding on the wooden door and urgent barking from the yard dog. Throwing on a thin, tattered padded cotton coat, he burst outside to find his grandfather, Uncle Shuisheng, holding a storm kerosene lantern, gazing toward the northern bank of the Wu River with a grim, pale face.\n\nPiercing through the boundless murk and whistling north wind, a lone kerosene torch on the stone steps of the abandoned northern dock was waving frantically up and down in desperate circles!\n\nImmediately after, carried by the freezing river wind, came faint, agonizing cries choked with frantic weeping:\n\n\"Crossing the river—! Lives are on the line—! Uncle Rong! Save us—!\"\n\nThe desperate pleas fluttered intermittently in the howls of the gale, yet struck like sledgehammers against the heart of every villager.\n\n\"It's the second daughter-in-law of the Lin family in Tong'ancuo!\" Uncle Shuisheng's face turned white as chalk, his voice trembling. \"She's nine months pregnant... word is the baby is breached and she's been hemorrhaging for two days straight! The northern highway was blocked dead by a rockslide—the county ambulance can't get through! They need to cross the river! Only our Xiwei side has ox carts that can take the back road through Fenyuan to the hospital in Changhua!\"\n\n\"B-but... the bridge is destroyed!\" Ah-Shun cried out in panic. \"The river is darker than ink right now, and the water is freezing enough to kill a man! Who could possibly cross?\"\n\n\"He can,\" Uncle Shuisheng turned toward the ferry crossing, awe shimmering in his gaze.\n\nWhen Uncle Shuisheng and Ah-Shun stumbled down to the dock with their lanterns, they saw a massive bamboo raft lashed to an ancient chinaberry tree by heavy hemp lines, rocking violently against the black, turbid current.\n\nUncle Rong was already aboard. He wore no palm cape, only a thin coarse-cloth work shirt, a hemp rescue rope tied around his waist, his lips shivering with a purplish tint. Yet his eagle eyes, illuminated by the kerosene lantern, gleamed as cold and sharp as twin drawn bayonets.\n\n\"Ah-Rong! You can't put in!\" The village warden, arriving in a panic, grabbed the hawser, weeping tears of terror. \"Winter mountain floods are the deadliest of all—the current is full of submerged logs and rolling ice! Going down there in pitch darkness is pure suicide!\"\n\n\"Even if it's suicide, I must go,\" Uncle Rong's voice showed not the slightest tremor, as unyielding as the bedrock of the river. \"Waving that torch in three circles is the signal for a life hanging by a thread. That's two human souls over there—a mother and a child who hasn't even seen the light of day. I, Lin Quanrong, accepted the township's appointment plaque. As long as there is breath in my lungs, this river will not sever a person's path to life!\"\n\nWith that, Uncle Rong drew the small billhook from his sash and cleanly severed the final thick mooring line tethered to the shore!\n\n\"Uncle Rong! I'm going with you!\"\n\nA crisp, resolute shout from the young boy detonated abruptly in the freezing gale.\n\nTwelve-year-old Ah-Shun lunged forward. Seizing the storm hurricane lantern from its wooden cradle with both hands, before the stunned gazes of the crowd, he leapt without a heartbeat of hesitation straight onto the violently tossing raft!\n\n\"Ah-Shun! Have you lost your mind?! Get off there!\" Uncle Shuisheng nearly lost his wits in terror, screaming from the bank.\n\n\"Grandpa, the northern bank is too dark to see! Uncle Rong needs both hands to punt the pole—there's no one to hold the lantern and spot the shoals for him!\" Ah-Shun bit through his lower lip, the salty tang of blood clearing his head instantly. Holding the lantern high above his head, his bare feet wedged firmly between the bamboo joints, he met Uncle Rong's astonished gaze. \"Uncle, you told me before—I am this boat's pole-guard! I am not afraid anymore!\"\n\nUncle Rong looked deeply into the young boy's eyes. He uttered not another word of dissuasion, only exhaling a heavy gust of white vapor from his nose: \"Good lad! Your spine has turned to steel! Hold that lantern steady—shine it three meters ahead along the waterline. The moment you spot black foam, shout!\"\n\n\"Boom—!\"\n\nThe thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole pushed off against the shoreline boulders with all Uncle Rong's might. The raft broke away instantly like an arrow loosed from a bow, plunging headlong into the pitch-black, freezing, bottomless expanse of the ink-dark river!\n\nExtreme, bone-piercing cold wrapped around the two of them instantly. The spray crashing into their faces was no longer the lukewarm mud of summer; it was freezing liquid ice, stinging their skin like thousands of shattered steel needles.\n\nAround them lay absolute, impenetrable darkness, save for the swaying lantern in Ah-Shun's hands, casting a fragile, solitary halo of yellow light across the churning black torrents.\n\n\"Uncle! Submerged log to our front-left!\" Ah-Shun screamed at the top of his lungs.\n\nBy the lantern's frail beam, Uncle Rong caught sight of a jagged, broken bamboo piling rolling beneath the surface. Channeling power from his hips, the veteran's pole stabbed lightning-fast into the riverbed, slipping the raft past the razor-sharp stake by inches.\n\nYet, just as the craft reached the absolute center of the deepest channel, the deadliest catastrophe struck!\n\nA massive boulder, swept down from the mountain highlands by the flash flood, rolled invisibly along the riverbed and smashed directly into the two auxiliary bamboo stalks at the stern!\n\n\"Crack—snap—!\"\n\nAccompanied by a deafening, splintering roar, the two colossal thorny bamboo trunks shattered into kindling. The taut galvanized binding snapped violently, and one flank of the raft tilted sharply downward. Freezing, pitch-black water surged frantically across the deck, sending the craft spinning into a terrifying downward vortex!\n\n\"The raft is splitting! We're going under!\" Ah-Shun lost his footing, crashing onto the slick bamboo skin, yet he curled his entire body to shield the frail lantern in his arms.\n\nIn that razor-thin margin between life and death, Uncle Rong performed an astonishing feat that Ah-Shun would carry to the end of his days.\n\nThe old ferryman shoved the Wave-Calming Pole into Ah-Shun's hands and bellowed: \"Ah-Shun, hold the pole tight—don't move!\"\n\nBefore the words had faded, Uncle Rong hurled himself over the side, plunging straight into the chest-deep, near-freezing black torrent!\n\n\"Uncle Rong—!\" Ah-Shun cried out in horror.\n\nIn that bone-numbing current, Uncle Rong resembled a deity forged of hardened steel. Bracing his shoulders and spine against the splintering bamboo stalks, he took the brunt of the icy surges slamming against his chest and head. His bare feet scraped frantically across the moss-covered reefs on the riverbed, carving bloody gashes into the ice-cold gravel with every desperate step!\n\n\"Hold... hold the pole! Ah-Shun... use... use the current to shove us toward the northern bank!\" Uncle Rong coughed out a ragged breath mingled with freezing spray, the veins in his neck bulging like gnarled tree roots, his gravelly roar shattering the frozen night.\n\nAh-Shun's face was drenched in a blur of tears and freezing river water, but he had no time to cry.\n\nThe twelve-year-old boy transformed every ounce of terror, shivering chill, and dread into raw, furious strength. Remembering the posture Uncle Rong had drilled into him countless times, he flexed his knees, thrusting the Wave-Calming Pole violently into the gravel bed below. Coordinating with the superhuman shove from Uncle Rong's flesh and bone beneath the surface, he leveraged the river's own savage momentum, shoving the sinking raft inch by inch, foot by foot, out of the clutches of the deadly whirlpool!\n\nFifty meters... thirty meters... ten meters!\n\n\"Caught it! We've got the line!\"\n\nWild shouts of ecstasy erupted from the men on the northern dock. A dozen burly villagers carrying torches plunged into the shallows, grabbing the hemp hawser flung from the bow and hauling the shattered craft up onto the gravel beach with all their might!\n\n\"Uncle Rong! Uncle Rong, how are you?!\"\n\nThe men scrambled into the shallows, lifting Uncle Rong—frozen stiff as iron, his lips and skin mottled a deep, suffocating purple—out of the icy water. The old man's shoulders and back were torn raw by splintered bamboo, the blood congealing into dark-red frost the moment it met the air. Yet his sunken eyes remained locked upon the thatched shelter atop the levee.\n\n\"The... the mother?\" Uncle Rong's teeth clattered uncontrollably, his voice barely a breath.\n\n\"In the ox cart! Wrapped in three layers of quilts! Uncle Rong, if you had arrived fifteen minutes later, both mother and child would have been gone!\" A burly farmer from Tong'ancuo dropped to his knees in the mud with a heavy thud, kowtowing deeply to Uncle Rong and Ah-Shun.\n\nTogether, the men lifted the unconscious mother onto the ox cart. The driver cracked his whip furiously, and the cart barreled down the muddy road toward Fenyuan and the hospital in Changhua.\n\nAt daybreak, a faint sliver of pale morning light appeared along the eastern horizon.\n\nThe furious winter rain finally ebbed, and a warm charcoal fire crackled within the thatched shelter on the northern bank. Ah-Shun sat by the hearth, clutching a piping bowl of hot ginger tea with both hands, watching Uncle Rong, wrapped in three heavy quilts, as the deadly pallor on his weathered face slowly gave way to a flush of life.\n\nJust then, along the highway leading back from Changhua, a bicycle came pedaling furiously toward them, the young rider shouting with ecstatic joy the entire way:\n\n\"Born! It's a boy! A big, healthy baby boy! Both mother and child are safe! They're safe!\"\n\nThat clear cry of good tidings echoed through the morning light, scattering the lingering chill across the entire breadth of the Wu River.\n\nTucked beneath his quilts, Uncle Rong slowly opened his eyes. Listening to the distant celebration, a deeply contented, radiant smile finally surfaced across his chapped, raw lips.\n\nAh-Shun turned his head, gazing across the river at the ruins of the hundred-meter bamboo bridge, now reduced to a few broken pilings, and then down at the fresh, bleeding calluses worn into his young palms.\n\nWithin his heart, the boy heard a sound with absolute clarity—the cry of life triumphing over the abyss, the burning solidarity of human beings watching over one another, and the indomitable soul that this wild, untamed river could never sweep away."},{"id":6,"rawContent":"# 第五章：大河退渡——最後一張手寫船票\n\n時光的流逝，有時比烏溪奔騰的濁浪還要迅猛。\n\n轉眼間，歲月的指針撥到了民國七十年代初。\n\n時代的巨輪轟隆隆碾過了這座長年寂靜的內陸孤島。下游幾十里外，宏偉的現代化公路與跨河大橋相繼拔地而起；寬闊平整的柏油路如同一條條黑色的緞帶，從彰化芬園與南投草屯一路向溪尾寮的邊緣延伸。\n\n村子裡原本安靜的泥土路面上，開始頻繁響起「轟隆隆」的野狼一二五機車引擎聲。\n\n村民們的生活習慣悄然改變了。年輕人買了摩托車，寧可多花二十分鐘繞道南邊的芬園大橋，經由省道繞進臺中市區，也不願再像父祖輩那樣，踩著黏膩的爛泥在渡口苦苦等待那艘搖晃微小的竹筏。\n\n就連孩子們的求學路也變了。溪尾國小的校舍與師資逐漸齊備，不再需要越區寄讀，加上家長們能騎機車載孩子繞路上學，昔日清晨六點那趟喧鬧緊張、滿載全村希望的「求學渡」，在不知不覺中成了泛黃泛舊的歷史陳跡。\n\n渡口的石階漸漸被厚厚的青苔與瘋長的蘆葦吞沒，一天到晚，常常連一個搭船的客人都等不到。\n\n秋風蕭瑟的午後，烏日鄉公所的一紙正式公文送到了渡口的老草棚裡。\n\n公文上的黑字冰冷而客氣——隨著全省公路交通網絡逐漸普及，渡口往來人潮銳減，為妥善運用公帑，自本月份起正式終止公聘擺渡人制度，溪尾渡口予以裁撤封渡。\n\n這意味著，林權榮老人長達三十餘年風雨無阻、守護全村出行的擺渡生涯，在今天走到了盡頭。\n\n今天是渡口營運的最後一天。\n\n夕陽如同一抹蒼涼的淡金，斜斜照在渡口那棵長滿粗糙樹皮的老苦楝樹下。\n\n年過古稀的阿榮伯獨自坐在那塊被磨得平滑的青石上。他的頭髮已經全白，宛如烏溪初冬落下的白霜；他那雙曾經能夠搏擊狂瀾的大手長滿了老年斑，皮膚乾癟鬆弛，但指節依然粗大如柴，靜靜握著那根立在身旁的五公尺大刺竹定波篙。\n\n在波光粼粼的岸邊，那艘陪伴了他半輩子的大竹筏，被最後一道晚霞染成了深沉的棗紅色。\n\n沒有熱鬧的道別儀式，也沒有鄉公所頒發的錦旗。村裡的老人們都在田裡忙著最後一茬二期稻作的除草，年輕人都進了城裡的工廠做工。大河依舊向西奔流，帶走了浪花，也帶走了一個世代的背影。\n\n阿榮伯從腰間摸出那隻磨破了邊緣的油布菸草袋，慢吞吞地捲了一支土菸，用火柴點燃。\n\n他深深吸了一口微苦的菸草，青白色的煙霧在暮色中緩緩散開。老人望著奔騰不息的江水，眼神裡沒有怨懟，只有一種平靜而深沉的眷戀。\n\n「嘟——嘟——！」\n\n突然，一陣短促、清脆而響亮的竹哨聲，自大堤頂端的防汛道路上驟然響起！\n\n阿榮伯渾身一震，捏著菸捲的手指猛地停在半空中。\n\n那是一把自製的竹雕哨子——那是多年前阿榮伯親手用大刺竹削成、送給那個在大浪中替他死死護住竹篙的少年的禮物！\n\n阿榮伯顫巍巍地站起身，手遮涼棚，瞇起混濁的雙眼朝著堤頂望去。\n\n只見在殘陽如血的天際線下，一道挺拔修長的身影正快步從堤頂走下石階。\n\n那是一位二十出頭的年輕人。他穿著一身漿洗得筆挺乾淨的白色棉布襯衫，腳踩一雙沾著些許塵土的棕色皮鞋，手裡提著一隻沉甸甸的黑色人造革公事包。他的身形像極了當年的水生伯，但鼻樑高聳、肩膀寬闊，眉宇間透著一股被現代知識與大江大河共同洗禮後的堅毅與從容。\n\n「阿順？！」阿榮伯嘴唇顫抖著，吐出了一個深埋在心底的名字。\n\n「阿榮伯！是我！阿順回來了！」\n\n李順安扔下公事包，三步並作兩步衝下長滿青苔的石階，像個孩子一樣撲到了老人的面前，一把緊緊握住了阿榮伯那雙冰涼乾枯的大手。\n\n當年的怯弱少年，如今已經是國立成功大學土木工程學系的高材生，以全系第一名的優異成績剛剛畢業，即將投身於臺灣重大基礎建設的國家工程隊！\n\n「你這孩子……」阿榮伯眼眶泛紅，粗糙的大手在阿順寬闊厚實的肩膀上用力拍了拍，「你阿公說你前幾天還在臺南忙著考工程師牌照，怎麼今天……跑回來了？」\n\n「阿公打電話到學校宿舍，說公所今天封渡。」阿順的眼眶微濕，聲音帶著一絲哽咽，「我是坐了火車到彰化，一路跑步趕回來的。阿榮伯，我怎麼能不回來？」\n\n說著，阿順鄭重地拉開胸前的襯衫口袋，從裡面小心翼翼地取出了一張折疊得整整齊齊的紅紙。\n\n那不是公所印製的公文，也不是市面上的車票，而是一張由十二歲的阿順當年用毛筆親手繪製的「手寫渡船票」。紅紙的正中央用工整的楷書寫著四個大字——「憑票過溪」，落款處還端端正正地按著一個小小的紅色手指印。\n\n「阿榮伯，三十多年來，全村老小坐您的船，公所貼錢，大家都免船票。」阿順雙手將紅紙捧過頭頂，深深向老人鞠了一躬，「但這張票，是阿順欠您的。今天，請您收下這張票，為我……再擺最後一次渡吧！」\n\n阿榮伯望著那張泛黃褪色的紅紙船票，望著上面稚嫩卻無比赤誠的筆跡，眼底積蓄已久的淚水，終於順著溝壑縱橫的臉頰無聲地滑落下來。\n\n老擺渡人伸出顫抖的手指，接過了那張輕如鴻毛、卻重逾千斤的紅紙，小心翼翼地夾進自己貼身的油布菸草袋裡。\n\n「好！好！阿伯今天……就收你這張票！」阿榮伯抹了一把臉上的淚水，挺直了微駝的脊梁，身上的老邁之氣在這一瞬間一掃而空，彷彿又變回了那個叱吒風浪的江上天神。\n\n「解纜！出港囉——！」\n\n一聲高亢悠長、穿透了三十年歲月的江上號子，再次在空曠蕭瑟的烏溪河灘上轟然炸響！\n\n阿順像少年時一樣，熟練地跳上船尾解開繫纜，抓起那支磨得發亮的木橈。\n\n阿榮伯單臂抱起那根伴隨了他一生的五公尺大刺竹定波篙，在岸邊卵石上沉沉一點。\n\n「嘩啦——」\n\n竹筏優雅而輕快地滑入了江心。\n\n沒有風暴，沒有山洪，夕陽將江面鋪成了一條流動的黃金之路。竹筏在微波中平穩地滑行著，發出輕柔的切水聲。\n\n「阿順，聽說你去城裡讀了洋書，學的是……造橋？」阿榮伯一邊悠悠撐著長篙，一邊望著平靜的江水問道。\n\n「是，阿榮伯。」阿順立在船尾，目光堅定地注視著眼前這條養育了他也考驗了他整整童年的大河，「在大學四年，我畫了上千張圖紙，算了幾萬道力學算式。我學的所有本領，都是為了這條河。」\n\n阿順抬起手，指向眼前這段最寬闊、最凶險的烏溪江面：「現在大家雖然可以繞道芬園，但繞一圈要多走十幾公里，遇到颱風山崩，溪尾依然會變成一座孤島。阿榮伯，您看著吧！將來，我一定會在這裡立起深深的橋墩，架起最寬的鋼樑，造一座哪怕百年一遇的大水也絕對沖不垮的『溪尾大橋』！」\n\n阿榮伯靜靜地聽著，蒼老的臉上露出了無比欣慰與釋然的笑容。\n\n「好啊……大橋好啊。」老人輕聲呢喃著，「阿伯老了，這雙手已經撐不動幾千斤的濁浪了。但是阿順，河面上的路不能斷，溪尾人的心不能散。」\n\n說話間，竹筏輕輕抵達了北堤渡口。\n\n那是這艘竹筏最後一次靠岸。\n\n阿榮伯走上前，雙手握住了那根長達五公尺、被歲月磨得通體油亮泛紅的大刺竹定波篙。\n\n老人深深凝視了它一眼，隨後轉過身，鄭重其事地將整根沉甸甸的竹篙，橫著端平，交到了年輕力壯的李順安手裡。\n\n「阿順，阿伯的船今天靠岸，以後不再出江了。」阿榮伯的目光深邃而肅穆，聲音如同江底湧動的暗潮，「這根定波篙，阿伯交給你。以後這條大河上的路，阿伯用竹筏擺渡了三十年；剩下的路，換你用大橋擺渡過去！」\n\n沉重的刺竹落入掌心，阿順的虎口微微一震。那上面依然帶著阿榮伯掌心滾燙的溫度，以及成千上萬次與急流卵石搏擊後留下的粗糙凹痕。\n\n阿順熱淚盈眶，雙手緊握長篙，向著老人深深跪拜下去：「阿伯放心！這根竹篙立在阿順心裡，這條大橋，我一定會為家鄉建起來！」\n\n夕陽徹底沉入大肚山後，夜幕降臨，天空中升起了滿天繁星。\n\n老擺渡人轉過身，步履緩慢卻無比踏實地走上了大堤；而在他的身後，年輕的工程師迎風而立，手握老刺竹篙，如同在暗夜的大河邊，立起了一座永不退色的燈塔。","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 5: The Great River Retires the Ferry—The Last Handwritten Ticket\n\nThe passage of time can sometimes surge even more swiftly than the turbid rapids of the Wu River.\n\nIn the blink of an eye, the hands of the clock turned to the early 1970s.\n\nThe colossal wheel of modern progress ground inexorably across this long-silent inland enclave. Dozens of miles downstream, grand modern highways and cross-river viaducts rose one after another from the soil; wide, level ribbons of asphalt stretched continuously from Fenyuan in Changhua and Caotun in Nantou, reaching right up to the thresholds of Xiwei Village.\n\nAlong the village's once-quiet dirt lanes, the guttural roar of Wild Wolf 125 motorcycle engines began to echo with increasing frequency.\n\nThe rhythms of daily life shifted quietly. Young men bought motorcycles, preferring to spend an extra twenty minutes detouring across the southern Fenyuan Bridge and taking provincial highways into downtown Taichung, rather than trudging through thick, sticky mire to wait for that fragile, rocking bamboo raft at the ferry crossing like their fathers and grandfathers had.\n\nEven the path to school had transformed. Facilities and teaching staff at Xiwei Elementary School gradually improved, ending the need to cross the river for out-of-district schooling, while parents shuttled their children along detour roads on motorbikes. Without anyone noticing, that frantic, tense 6:00 AM \"School Crossing\"—once laden with the hopes of the entire community—slowly faded into a yellowed relic of history.\n\nThe stone stairs of the ferry landing were gradually swallowed by thick green moss and overgrown wild reeds. From dawn to dusk, days would pass without a single passenger stepping aboard.\n\nOn a bleak, wind-swept autumn afternoon, an official dispatch from the Wuri Township Office was delivered to the weather-beaten thatched shelter at the dock.\n\nThe black characters on the document were polite yet chillingly final: with the nationwide expansion of modern highway networks and the steep decline in ferry passenger traffic, in order to properly steward public funds, the officially subsidized ferryman system was to be formally discontinued starting this month, and the Xiwei Ferry Crossing decommissioned for good.\n\nThis meant that Uncle Rong's career of guarding the village's lifeline across the water through wind and storm—spanning more than thirty years—had reached its end today.\n\nToday was the final day of the ferry's operation.\n\nThe setting sun cast a pale, mournful wash of gold angling beneath the ancient chinaberry tree, illuminating its coarse, furrowed bark.\n\nUncle Rong, now past seventy, sat alone on the smooth, worn bluestone slab. His hair was entirely snow-white, resembling the early-winter frost that settled over the Wu River. The great hands that had once wrestled roaring whirlpools were covered with age spots, his skin loose and withered. Yet his knuckles remained as thick and sinewy as seasoned timber, resting quietly against the five-meter thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole standing beside him.\n\nAlong the shimmering shoreline, the great raft that had accompanied him for half a lifetime was dyed a deep, somber crimson by the last rays of twilight.\n\nThere was no festive farewell ceremony, nor any silk commemorative banner bestowed by the township office. The village elders were busy weeding the final crop of the second rice season in the paddies; the young people had all gone off to work in urban factories. The great river continued its relentless westward race, carrying away whitecaps, and carrying away the silhouette of an entire era.\n\nFrom his sash, Uncle Rong fished out his frayed, oiled-cloth tobacco pouch. Methodically, he rolled a pinch of rustic tobacco and struck a match.\n\nDrawing a deep puff of bitter smoke, a pale plume dispersed slowly into the gathering dusk. The old man gazed upon the roaring waters, his eyes devoid of bitterness—only a quiet, bottomless affection.\n\n\"Toot—toot—!\"\n\nSuddenly, a crisp, sharp, piercing burst from a bamboo whistle echoed down from the flood levee path above!\n\nUncle Rong's entire frame jolted. His fingers holding the cigarette froze mid-air.\n\nIt was a hand-carved bamboo whistle—a gift Uncle Rong had whittled from a piece of wild thorny bamboo many years ago, given to that very boy who had held the pole for dear life amidst the raging waves!\n\nTrembling, the old man rose to his feet. Shading his brow, he narrowed his clouded eyes to peer up at the crest of the embankment.\n\nSilhouetted against the blood-red horizon, a tall, upright figure was striding briskly down the mossy stone steps.\n\nIt was a young man in his early twenties. He wore an immaculately washed and pressed white cotton shirt, brown leather shoes dusted with the road, and carried a heavy black faux-leather briefcase. His frame strongly resembled Uncle Shuisheng in his youth, but with a high bridge to his nose and broad shoulders, his brow radiated a calm, unyielding resolve tempered by modern science and wild waters alike.\n\n\"Ah-Shun?!\" Uncle Rong's lips quivered as he breathed out a name buried deep in his heart.\n\n\"Uncle Rong! It's me! Ah-Shun has come home!\"\n\nDropping his briefcase, Li Shun'an cleared the mossy steps three at a time. Like a boy returning to his sanctuary, he rushed before the old man, grasping Uncle Rong's cold, withered hands tightly in his own.\n\nThe once-timid child had become a top graduate from the Department of Civil Engineering at National Cheng Kung University. Having graduated first in his class, he was about to join the national engineering corps dedicated to Taiwan's major infrastructure developments!\n\n\"You silly child...\" Uncle Rong's eyes reddened. His rough palm thumped heavily against Ah-Shun's broad, solid shoulder. \"Your grandpa said you were in Tainan taking your professional engineer licensing exams just a couple days ago... why did you come running back today?\"\n\n\"Grandpa phoned my dorm, saying the township office was closing the crossing today,\" Ah-Shun's eyes glistened with tears, his voice catching in his throat. \"I took the train to Changhua and ran all the way home on foot. Uncle Rong, how could I not come back?\"\n\nWith that, Ah-Shun carefully unbuttoned his shirt pocket and pulled out a neatly folded square of red paper.\n\nIt was no official pass issued by the government, nor a printed transit ticket. It was a \"Handwritten Ferry Ticket\" brushed meticulously by a twelve-year-old Ah-Shun with ink and hair-pen all those years ago. In the center of the red paper, four neat characters read: \"Valid for River Crossing.\" In the lower corner, a small, faded red thumbprint was stamped squarely into the fiber.\n\n\"Uncle Rong, for over thirty years, every soul in our village rode your boat for free because the township footed the bill,\" Ah-Shun raised the red paper above his head with both hands, bowing deeply before the elder. \"Yet this ticket is what Ah-Shun has owed you all along. Today, please accept this ticket, and ferry me across... for one last time!\"\n\nUncle Rong looked at the faded red paper ticket, taking in those earnest, youthful brushstrokes. The tears gathered in his eyes finally spilled over, trickling down the deep ravines carved into his weathered cheeks.\n\nThe old ferryman reached out a trembling hand, accepting that slip of paper—light as a feather, yet heavier than a thousand tons of stone—and tucked it reverently into the oiled-cloth tobacco pouch next to his chest.\n\n\"Good! Splendid! Today... Uncle will accept your ticket!\" Uncle Rong wiped the wetness from his face and straightened his stooped spine. The frailty of his years seemed to vanish in an instant, as if he had transformed once more into that river god who commanded the tempest.\n\n\"Cast off! We're putting out to water—!\"\n\nA resonant, prolonged river cadence that had pierced thirty years of wind and storm detonated across the barren gravel beaches of the Wu River once more!\n\nJust as he had done as a boy, Ah-Shun sprang expertly onto the stern to untie the mooring line, seizing the polished wooden paddle.\n\nUncle Rong tucked the five-meter thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole under his arm, planting it down against the shoreline stones with a resounding thud.\n\n\"Swish—\"\n\nThe bamboo raft glided gracefully and effortlessly into midstream.\n\nThere was no storm, no flash flood. The evening sun paved the river into a shimmering highway of liquid gold. The raft skimmed across the ripples with absolute serenity, parting the surface with gentle whispers.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, I heard you went to the city to study foreign books, learning to... build bridges?\" Uncle Rong asked softly, looking out across the tranquil waters as he steered with ease.\n\n\"Yes, Uncle Rong,\" Ah-Shun stood at the stern, his gaze locked unblinkingly upon the river that had nurtured and tested his entire childhood. \"In four years of university, I drafted thousands of blueprints and calculated tens of thousands of structural formulas. Every ounce of knowledge I mastered was for the sake of this river.\"\n\nAh-Shun lifted his hand, pointing toward the widest, most treacherous reach of the channel before them: \"Right now, folks can detour through Fenyuan, but that adds more than a dozen kilometers. When typhoons trigger mudslides, Xiwei will still become an isolated island. Uncle Rong, mark my words! One day, I will drive deep bridge piers right here, hoist the widest steel girders, and construct a Xiwei Bridge that even a hundred-year flood can never wash away!\"\n\nUncle Rong listened in silence, an expression of boundless peace and vindication blooming across his aged features.\n\n\"Splendid... a great bridge is splendid indeed,\" the old man murmured softly. \"Uncle has grown old; these hands can no longer wrestle thousands of pounds of brown rapids. But Ah-Shun, the road across this water must never break, and the hearts of the Xiwei people must never scatter.\"\n\nAs they spoke, the raft bumped gently against the stone steps of the northern dock.\n\nIt was the final landing this raft would ever make.\n\nUncle Rong stepped forward, taking that five-meter-long thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole—polished to a deep, glossy chestnut by the passage of time—into both hands.\n\nThe old man gazed at it intently for a lingering moment. Then, turning around, he brought the heavy pole level across his palms, presenting it solemnly to the strong, capable hands of Li Shun'an.\n\n\"Ah-Shun, Uncle's boat has reached the shore today; it will never venture onto the water again,\" Uncle Rong's eyes were bottomless and sacred, his voice rumbling like the deep undercurrents of the riverbed. \"Uncle entrusts this Wave-Calming Pole to you. For thirty years, I ferried our people across this great river on a bamboo raft; for the road that remains, it will be your turn to ferry them across with a great bridge!\"\n\nAs the heavy thorny bamboo settled into his grip, Ah-Shun felt a resonant tremor through his palms. The pole still retained the fiery heat of Uncle Rong's touch, bearing the coarse scars from tens of thousands of battles against rapids and boulders.\n\nTears blurred Ah-Shun's vision. Clasping the long pole firmly with both hands, he fell to his knees in deep reverence before the elder: \"Rest easy, Uncle! This pole stands planted within Ah-Shun's heart. This great bridge—I swear I will build it for our home!\"\n\nAs the sun sank completely behind Mount Dadu, twilight yielded to night, and a canopy of brilliant stars adorned the sky.\n\nThe old ferryman turned and walked slowly yet steadily up the long earthen dike. Behind him, the young engineer stood tall against the evening breeze, gripping the ancient bamboo pole—like an eternal lighthouse erected beside the dark river, never to fade."},{"id":7,"rawContent":"# 第六章：尾聲・橋起穗香——擺渡人的食光穗稻\n\n二○一六年秋天，一個足以載入溪尾寮百年史冊的晴朗日子。\n\n烏溪兩岸彩旗招展，萬人空巷。\n\n歷時多年規劃與艱苦施工、耗資數億元打造的「溪尾大橋」正式舉行通車典禮。\n\n紅白相間的鋼拱橋梁宛如一條騰空飛躍的長虹，以傲然之姿橫跨在寬達數百公尺的烏溪之上。深深扎入河床岩盤的巨大沉箱與耐震防洪橋墩，足以抵禦百年一遇的狂暴山洪。過去需要冒險搭乘竹筏、或是騎車繞道南投彰化近一小時的漫長險途，如今開車只要短短五分鐘，就能將溪尾與臺中市烏日核心區緊緊縫合在一起。\n\n在通車典禮的剪綵隊伍前方，站著一位頭髮花白、身形挺拔威嚴的長者。\n\n那是李順安。經過數十年的磨礪，他早已是名震全臺的資深橋樑工程專家。在規劃與推動溪尾大橋建設的漫長歲月裡，他無數次頂著烈日站在滾燙的河灘上測繪水文、勘定地質。\n\n剪綵結束後，人群漸漸散去。李順安獨自一人緩步走在平整開闊的六線道新橋面上。\n\n他停在主橋拱的最高處，扶著雪亮的鋼製護欄，向下俯瞰。\n\n幾十公尺深處的烏溪水，依舊奔騰向西流淌。在現代化的巨型橋墩旁，隱約還能看見早年那座廢棄渡口的殘破石階，以及那一棵在秋風中落葉紛飛的百年老苦楝樹。\n\n老人從風衣內側口袋裡，掏出了一張用透明塑膠膜封存得完好無損的泛黃紅紙——那是四十多年前，阿榮伯臨終前託人交還給他的「手寫船票」。紅紙上的字跡依然清晰，小小的紅色指印依然鮮豔如初。\n\n李順安望著奔流的江水，眼眶溫熱，對著滔滔水聲輕聲呢喃：\n\n「阿榮伯，您看見了嗎？阿順沒有食言。咱們溪尾的大橋……終於立起來了！」\n\n江風掠過橋拱，發出宛如竹哨般的悠遠低鳴，彷彿是老船夫在長河彼岸傳來的欣慰回應。\n\n然而，大橋通了，孤島的宿命被終結了，一個新的擔憂卻在阿順心中悄悄升起——\n\n現代交通的迅捷便利，讓年輕人紛紛搬進了都市的水泥叢林；寬闊的柏油路帶來了車流，卻也帶走了昔日田埂間的溫情。那些關於擺渡人的勇氣、竹便橋的守望相助，以及黑土裡結出黃金稻穗的感恩，難道就要隨著老一輩的凋零，徹底被現代文明遺忘嗎？\n\n這個問題的答案，在幾年後由他的女兒林曉棠給了出來。\n\n二○二一年，隨著「溪尾二橋」跨越貓羅溪、貫通彰化與南投，溪尾寮徹底成為了串連三縣市的核心樞紐。\n\n剛從都市辭去行銷策劃高薪工作的林曉棠，帶著滿滿的創意與對家鄉深沉的眷戀回到了溪尾村。\n\n「爸，大橋連起了兩岸的路，但我們要用這片土地的記憶，連起新一代孩子的心。」曉棠站在金黃色的稻田邊，堅定地對父親說道。\n\n在烏日區農會與溪尾國小的全力支持下，曉棠以曾祖輩水生伯的「黑土沃田」、阿榮伯的「擺渡人傳奇」，以及父親阿順「護篙建橋」的故事為核心靈魂，創辦了名揚全臺的食農文化教育品牌——「擺渡人的食光穗稻」。\n\n她將老阿公當年種植的優質稻米推廣改良為全國知名的冠軍香米（臺稉十六號與高雄一四七號）；她把河床沙地的西瓜採收設計成親子體驗；更重要的是，她把整條烏溪的百年水文歷史，變成了孩子們腳下最生動的戶外課堂。\n\n這項創新計畫一舉榮獲了農委會「全國十大食農體驗路線」的殊榮。\n\n時光悄悄回到了序曲中的那個深秋午後。\n\n「曉棠姊姊，那我們今天也要像阿順爺爺小時候一樣，學著怎麼在大水裡撐竹篙嗎？」\n\n圍在老刺竹定波篙旁的溪尾國小孩子們，聽完了整段跨越半世紀的大河傳奇，一個個眼睛發亮，神情激動不已。\n\n「當然要！」曉棠笑著一揮手，「不過不是在大河裡冒險，而是在咱們學校旁邊經過整治的生態水圳裡！大家跟我來！」\n\n在微風輕拂的水圳邊，幾艘由烏日竹藝師傅依照古法紮製、兩頭微微翹起的小竹筏已經穩穩泊在清澈平緩的水面上。\n\n孩子們脫掉球鞋，換上了藺草編織的防滑草鞋，在曉棠與學校老師的指導下，排著隊、踩著節奏依次登上了竹筏。\n\n平頭男孩小心翼翼地雙手握住那根被磨得油亮光滑的老刺竹定波篙。\n\n「站直！雙腳與肩同寬，膝蓋微微彎曲！」平頭男孩學著故事裡阿榮伯的口訣，大聲喊了出來。\n\n「看哪裡？」後面的孩子們齊聲問道。\n\n「看遠方的大肚山！」\n\n孩子們爆發出一陣清脆歡暢的笑聲。竹篙輕輕一點水底，竹筏在水圳中劃開了一道道美麗的漣漪，穩穩前行。\n\n在水圳旁邊的廣袤水田裡，另一群孩子正戴著竹編斗笠，赤著雙腳深陷在溫暖肥沃的沖積黑土中。他們手裡握著安全的小鐮刀，跟著滿臉慈祥笑容的老農夫們，親手割下一把把金黃沉甸、散發著濃郁米香的稻穗。\n\n收割結束後，孩子們圍坐在田埂邊的老苦楝樹蔭下，迫不及待地打開了散發著蒸騰熱氣的「擺渡人割稻竹編便當」。\n\n便當裡裝著剛出鍋、散發著淡淡天然芋頭香氣的頂級烏溪冠軍白米飯，上面鋪滿了用甘甜醬汁慢火煨煮的滷五花肉、金黃酥脆的菜脯蛋，以及切得厚厚的、翠綠爽口的涼拌高瓜。\n\n「哇！這米飯嚼起來又香又甜，就算不配菜也能吃三碗！」\n\n「這就是剛才曉棠姊姊說的，吸飽了烏溪營養的中央山脈黑土種出來的米啊！」\n\n孩子們大口大口地扒著飯，嘴角沾著飯粒，臉上洋溢著無比純真滿足的笑容。\n\n在田埂的高處，七十多歲的李順安拄著拐杖，身旁站著女兒林曉棠。父女倆靜靜望著眼前這幅生機勃勃的景象。\n\n遠處，宏偉的溪尾大橋上車水馬龍，那是現代文明飛速前行的強大動脈；近處，金黃色的稻浪在秋風中如波濤般翻滾不息，孩子們的歡聲笑語在田野間久久迴盪。\n\n那一根長達五公尺、歷經無數狂瀾洗禮的大刺竹定波篙，正安安靜靜地立在田埂中央。它的身上落滿了金黃色的稻殼，在陽光下泛著溫暖而永恆的光芒。\n\n大河奔流，大橋飛跨；擺渡人的木槳雖然停了，但那份深植於黑土之中的勇氣、慈悲與敬畏，早已化作了無邊無際的稻浪，代代相傳，生生不息。","rawContentEn":"# Chapter 6: Epilogue: A Bridge Rises with Fragrant Grain—The Ferryman's Golden Grain\n\nIn the autumn of 2016, a radiant morning arrived that would be etched forever into the hundred-year history of Xiwei Village.\n\nBanners fluttered along both banks of the Wu River, and crowds turned out by the thousands.\n\nFollowing years of meticulous planning, arduous construction, and an investment of hundreds of millions of dollars, the Xiwei Bridge was inaugurated with grand ceremonies.\n\nThe red-and-white steel-arch structure soared like a magnificent rainbow, bridging the broad Wu River with monumental pride. Its massive caissons and earthquake-resilient flood dikes, anchored deep into the riverbed bedrock, were engineered to withstand once-in-a-century catastrophic deluges. A grueling journey that once required braving the rapids on a fragile bamboo raft—or spending nearly an hour detouring on motorbikes through Nantou and Changhua—could now be crossed in a mere five minutes by car, seamlessly stitching Xiwei to the vibrant heart of Taichung's Wuri District.\n\nAt the very front of the ribbon-cutting procession stood an elder with silvered hair, his posture tall and commanding.\n\nThat was Li Shun'an. Tempered by decades of professional toil, he had long become a renowned authority in bridge engineering across Taiwan. Over the years dedicated to planning and driving the construction of the Xiwei Bridge, he had stood countless times under the blazing sun upon these scorching gravel bars, surveying hydrology and mapping geological faults.\n\nAfter the ribbon-cutting concluded, the bustling crowds gradually dispersed. Li Shun'an walked slowly and alone across the vast, smooth deck of the six-lane bridge.\n\nHe stopped at the highest apex of the main steel arch, resting his hands upon the gleaming railing to peer down.\n\nDozens of meters below, the waters of the Wu River still raced relentlessly westward. Beside the colossal modern piers, one could still catch faint glimpses of the mossy, broken stone steps of the long-abandoned ferry dock, and that centennial chinaberry tree scattering autumn leaves in the river breeze.\n\nFrom inside his trench coat pocket, the elder produced a yellowed slip of red paper, immaculately preserved within a sealed protective film. It was that \"Handwritten Ferry Ticket\" that Uncle Rong had entrusted to someone to return to him before passing away forty years earlier. The brushstrokes remained unmistakable, and that tiny red thumbprint was as vivid as the day it was pressed.\n\nGazing at the roaring currents, his eyes grew warm with unshed tears. Leaning into the wind, Li Shun'an whispered softly to the river:\n\n\"Uncle Rong, do you see it? Ah-Shun did not break his word. Our Xiwei Bridge... has finally risen!\"\n\nThe river wind whistling through the steel arches emitted a resonant hum, like a distant bamboo whistle answering him from the far bank of eternity.\n\nYet, while the bridge was built and the enclave's isolation conquered, a quiet sorrow had begun to stir within Ah-Shun's heart.\n\nThe swift convenience of modern transit had drawn the younger generation away into the concrete jungles of the cities; broad ribbons of asphalt brought traffic, yet also swept away the warmth once shared along the earthen field ridges. Would the ferryman's courage, the solidarity of the bamboo footbridge, and the profound reverence for the golden grains nurtured by the river's black silt be forgotten as the older generation faded away?\n\nThe answer to this question was delivered a few years later by his daughter, Lin Xiaotang.\n\nIn 2021, with the opening of the Xiwei Second Bridge spanning the Maoluo River to connect Changhua and Nantou directly, Xiwei Village formally became the vital crossroads linking three counties.\n\nHaving resigned from a lucrative marketing executive career in the city, Lin Xiaotang returned to Xiwei Village brimming with bold ideas and a bottomless affection for her roots.\n\n\"Dad, the bridge connects the roads between the shores, but we must use the memories of this land to connect the hearts of the new generation,\" Xiaotang said resolutely, standing beside the shimmering paddies.\n\nBacked by the full support of the Wuri District Farmers' Association and Xiwei Elementary School, Xiaotang crafted a nationally celebrated agricultural-cultural brand named \"The Ferryman's Golden Grain,\" centered upon Great-Grandpa Shuisheng's fertile black earth, Uncle Rong's legendary ferry, and her father Ah-Shun's legacy of the Wave-Calming Pole and the great bridge.\n\nShe promoted and refined the heirloom rice into nationally crowned champion varieties (Taikeng 16 and Kaohsiung 147); she turned the harvesting of sandbar watermelons into interactive family experiences; and most importantly, she transformed the century-old hydrology of the Wu River into a living, outdoor classroom beneath the children's feet.\n\nThis visionary initiative was formally honored as one of the Council of Agriculture's \"Top Ten Agro-Tourism Experience Routes\" nationwide.\n\nTime softly looped back to that late autumn afternoon introduced in the Prologue.\n\n\"Sister Xiaotang, are we going to learn how to punt a bamboo raft across roaring waters just like Grandpa Ah-Shun did when he was little?\"\n\nThe children of Xiwei Elementary School, gathered around the ancient thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole, finished listening to the half-century saga with shining eyes and thumping hearts.\n\n\"Of course we are!\" Xiaotang smiled, gesturing with a sweep of her arm. \"Though not by risking our lives in the wild river, but right here in our school's revitalized ecological canal! Follow me, everyone!\"\n\nAlong the tranquil canal caressed by a gentle breeze, several small bamboo rafts—lashed in the ancient style by master craftsmen with slightly upturned bows—floated steadily atop the crystal-clear waters.\n\nShedding their sneakers, the children pulled on non-slip sandals woven from rushes. Guided by Xiaotang and the teachers, they stepped aboard in disciplined rhythm, one by one.\n\nThe buzz-cut boy carefully clasped the glossy, chestnut-burnished Wave-Calming Pole with both hands.\n\n\"Stand tall! Feet shoulder-width apart, knees slightly flexed!\" he bellowed at the top of his lungs, reciting Uncle Rong's mantra from the story.\n\n\"Where should we look?\" the children behind him chorused in unison.\n\n\"Look at Mount Dadu in the distance!\"\n\nThe children burst into a cascade of crisp, joyful laughter. With a gentle push of the pole against the canal bed, the raft parted the water into shimmering ripples, gliding smoothly forward.\n\nIn the vast paddies beside the canal, another group of children wearing conical bamboo hats squatted barefoot in the warm, rich alluvial black mud. Clasping child-safe sickles alongside grandfatherly farmers wearing beaming smiles, they harvested golden, drooping bunches of rice that filled the air with sweet fragrance.\n\nWhen the harvest ended, the children gathered beneath the shade of an ancient chinaberry tree along the ridge, eagerly unwrapping their steaming \"Ferryman's Harvest Bamboo Lunchboxes.\"\n\nInside rested freshly steamed, naturally taro-scented champion rice from the Wu River granary, topped with slow-braised pork belly glistening in sweet soy reduction, crisp golden preserved radish omelets, and thick slices of refreshing, cold-tossed local luffa.\n\n\"Wow! This rice is so fragrant and naturally sweet, I could eat three bowls without any side dishes!\"\n\n\"This is the very rice Sister Xiaotang talked about—grown in the black silt from the Central Mountain Range, packed with all the nutrition of the river!\"\n\nThe children shoveled the rice hungrily into their mouths, grains clinging to the corners of their lips, their faces glowing with pure, unadulterated happiness.\n\nStanding atop the high earthen ridge, seventy-something Li Shun'an rested on his cane, his daughter Lin Xiaotang at his side. Father and daughter gazed quietly upon the vibrant scene unfolding before them.\n\nIn the distance, traffic streamed across the grand arches of the Xiwei Bridge—the thrumming artery of modern civilization racing ahead. Near at hand, golden rice waves rolled endlessly beneath the autumn wind, while the joyful laughter of children echoed across the fields.\n\nStanding silently in the center of the ridge was that five-meter thorny bamboo Wave-Calming Pole, seasoned by countless tempests. Sprinkled with golden husks of harvested rice, it radiated a warm, eternal gleam beneath the afternoon sun.\n\nThe great river rolls on; the great bridge soars high. Though the ferryman's paddle has rested, the courage, compassion, and reverence rooted within this black earth have transformed into an endless sea of golden grain—passed down from generation to generation, blossoming without end."}];
